/* Generated by app/tools/render_project_tutorials.py from maintained docs.
 * Read-only lesson content, not project assets or runtime data. Do not hand edit. */
Forge.services.ProjectLessonData = Object.freeze({"format": "NESForgeProjectLessons", "version": 1, "chapters": [{"id": "hosted", "title": "Hosted Worlds and shared editor workflow", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"hosted-hosted-worlds-shared-editor-localhost-workflow\">Hosted Worlds: shared-editor localhost workflow\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>The hosted editor is a second build of the canonical NES Forge editor. Its\nnative import/edit/build paths retain the ordinary schema and registered native\nwriters. The hosted build adds opt-in World publication, source inspection and\nplayer tools. Host protocol 13 is separate from native project schema versions.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"hosted-open-and-run\">Open and run\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Extract the complete kit. Keep its directory structure.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open \u003ccode>nes-forge.html\u003c/code> for native/offline work, or the hosted HTML under\n\u003ccode>experiments/hosted-worlds/v17.1\u003c/code> for WORLD Live tools.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For hosting, install Python 3.11+, then \u003ccode>python -m pip install aiohttp pycryptodome\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Start the host with that version folder\u2019s Windows or macOS/Linux launcher.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open the hosted HTML, load a supported Zelda II native World and choose its\nintended region. WORLD Live \u2192 enter the token printed by this host process \u2192\nTest localhost. It should verify protocol 13.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Load selected World on host after reviewing the explicit publication prompt.\nThis publishes a snapshot; it does not patch the source ROM. Open the player\nclient at the local address printed by the host, using separate browser profiles\nand test wallets for separate players.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preserve the private \u003ccode>host/localhost-world.db\u003c/code> if continuing a session. Stop both\nprocesses before moving a database between kit folders. Keep the runtime host,\nplayer and editor from the same version together.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"hosted-editing-owner-and-impact\">Editing owner and impact\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Data\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Owner\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Result\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native source, terrain, CHR, poses, build recipes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing canonical editor services\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Save Project As / bounded native ROM build\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Published World terrain/art snapshot\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Explicit editor export, validated host import\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Multiplayer World revision\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Player identity, XP, camps, shared actions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Local host and its database/session state\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Server-authoritative multiplayer state\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Recovered room records, Link art and traces\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Verified local source plus read-only recovery\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Evidence/display; gameplay fidelity needs separate verification\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>Use the native project\u2019s Native tools and preflight for ROM edits. Use explicit\nsafe terrain publication for a compatible hosted terrain change; a complete World\npublication has a different revision/reset scope and asks for confirmation.\nCurrent native editors, Resources and Retro Workstation remain available in\nboth builds. Punch-Out pose/art work continues through its own native writers.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"hosted-rebuild-or-change-html\">Rebuild or change HTML\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Edit shared editor modules under \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code>. The default manifest builds the native\neditor; \u003ccode>app/hosted-source-manifest.json\u003c/code> adds eleven extension modules from the\nsame tree. Run the default bundler, root-entry synchronizer, then the hosted\nmanifest build. Each output has its own build-input inventory. Avoid editing a\nsecond source snapshot; regenerate both outputs after a shared change. Direct\nHTML experiments should be made in a separate copy and ported to modular source\nwhen retained. Host/client source remains under the versioned experiment folder.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"hosted-current-limits\">Current limits\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Hosted movement, attack zones, damage and some traversal are experimental. Original\nLink pixels and source selectors do not establish native Zelda II combat, enemies,\nspells or physics parity. Wallet authentication does not mint items or deploy a\nblockchain. The host binds to localhost; public internet deployment is separate\nwork. Source ROMs, game media, player databases, wallet keys and admin tokens are\nnot part of the repository or kit.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/HOSTED_WORLDS.md", "sourceSha256": "646c0f3384ca4ee0711b665699c360fb126fd1d2e9ce4962ce45bd1ded25bc3d"}, {"id": "projects", "title": "Title methods and tutorial requirements", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"projects-per-project-recovery-tutorials\">Per-project recovery tutorials\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Each supported title needs its own reproducible tutorial. The common NES Forge guide explains the editors and architecture; a title tutorial explains that game's revision, storage formats, bank context, editable fields and rebuild method. Importing a title does not establish support for every asset or a complete game.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>This documentation requirement applies to subsequent recovery checkpoints. It does not retroactively certify older adapters. The current worked title is Punch-Out. Earlier titles have the starting workflows and evidence listed below; expanding them into full lessons remains recorded work.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects-read-in-this-order\">Read in this order\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Read the \u003ca href=\"#general\">developer and asset workflow guide\u003c/a> for the full asset map, independent RE, 6502, external hex editing and HTML changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read the title's own tutorial. \u003ca href=\"#punch-out\">Punch-Out\u003c/a> is the current worked example, with separate native editing, source-reference and standalone authoring paths.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the \u003ca href=\"#template\">tutorial template\u003c/a> when adding another title or revising an existing one.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Consult the title's verification reports and repeat the relevant workflow on your own input. Checkpoint evidence is dated; it is not evidence for a later changed application.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>PROJECT_HANDBOOK.html\u003c/code> in the parent documentation directory combines these lessons and the general guide into one offline reading file. The HTML is documentation. The NES Forge application remains a separate standalone HTML, saved projects are separate JSON files, and generated games are separate \u003ccode>.nes\u003c/code> files.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To regenerate the reading file from the repository root, run \u003ccode>python3 app/tools/render_project_tutorials.py\u003c/code> with the Python \u003ccode>markdown2\u003c/code> package available. This optional documentation dependency is not part of the application runtime. The renderer embeds the existing title notes and the full synthetic codec source; it does not embed game assets.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects-current-title-methods-and-starting-workflows\">Current title methods and starting workflows\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The table distinguishes actual installed paths. It is a documentation inventory, not a fresh all-title acceptance run. Use the exact investigated revision or an explicitly admitted derivative. Names and mapper numbers alone do not establish a match.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Title\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Start and make one bounded edit\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Why its tutorial differs\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Tutorial/evidence status\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Mike Tyson's Punch-Out!!, US PRG0/PRG1\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open ROM / project \u2192 prepare recovered resources \u2192 import; Recovered content \u2192 Open ring and audience; paint supported tiles/attribute IDs, use dedicated native CHR/fight-palette or Glass Joe full/incremental position views with a grouped idle option, or run the bounded sequence; save/reopen and Build &amp; Run\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>MMC2 pattern-read latches; two compressed arena pages; opponents are sprites, Mac/referee are background commands; full and incremental poses have separate placement records; reference copies have different ownership; knockdown/count/round experiments record explicit temporary RAM seeds\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"#punch-out\">Full current playbook\u003c/a>; \u003ccode>reviews/punch-out-incremental-pose-1/\u003c/code> and preceding title evidence\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Metroid, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import recovered resources; Area library \u2192 Investigated source \u2192 area/room; NATIVE ROOM STRUCTURES \u2192 existing object \u2192 replacement structure \u2192 Replace selected structure\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Ordered structures, fixed positions/palettes and overlapping attribute effects; replace a whole source structure rather than a Scene pixel\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"#metroid\">Room steps\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#metroid-audio\">Audio steps\u003c/a>; full lesson expansion queued\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Adventures of Lolo, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import; choose source room 1; NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS \u2192 column 2, row 2 \u2192 Floor \u2192 Replace selected cell; save/reopen/build/reimport\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bottom-up compressed rows in CHR-backed storage; limited terrain codes and interior cells; protected actor/settings cells; fixed per-room capacity\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"#lolo\">Room acceptance\u003c/a>, \u003ca href=\"#lolo-audio\">Audio acceptance\u003c/a>; full lesson expansion queued\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>River City Ransom, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import; select an available street segment in Area library; substitute an existing native tile with NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS; check shared appearances, save/reopen/build/reimport\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Shared physical row bytes; 20-row background crop; interior/special screens use another path and remain unavailable\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"#river-city\">Scene and Audio steps\u003c/a>; full lesson expansion queued\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Shadowgate, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import; select an available picture; substitute an original tile in NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS; save/reopen/build/reimport\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A 112\u00d7112 picture crop, not a complete room; objects, doors, alternate states and puzzle logic are separate\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ca href=\"#shadowgate\">Picture and Audio steps\u003c/a>; full lesson expansion queued\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Mega Man, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import; select an available source-room page in Area library; replace an existing 32\u00d732 native block using NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Column-major physical room storage; shared pointer slots; initial stage graphics differ from runtime transitions; 256-pixel room height spans views\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-megaman-resources.js\u003c/code>; full tutorial and current owner acceptance remain queued; this is Mega Man, separate from the Mega Man 2 Audio profile\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Contra, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import; select an available level/table slot and page; use NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS to replace a source block; save/reopen/build/reimport\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compressed 32\u00d732 blocks, row-copy commands, initial CHR-RAM loads; alternate graphics contexts can refuse\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-prg-resources.js\u003c/code>; full tutorial/current title-specific acceptance queued; no matching Contra Audio profile is installed in this catalog\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Final Fantasy, two investigated US inputs\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare/import; choose standard map, palette context and page; replace a native 16\u00d716 block with NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>64\u00d764 compressed standard maps; normal/room palette views share a map; 256\u00d7240 view boundaries can split blocks; this path does not establish overworld support\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-prg-resources.js\u003c/code>; full tutorial/current title-specific acceptance queued; no matching Final Fantasy Audio profile is installed in this catalog\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>TMNT, investigated Area 1 input\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare the supported native layout project; select a source screen; NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS \u2192 existing 32\u00d732 block \u2192 replace cell\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bounded Area 1 side-view screen table, not the complete game; native block definitions and attributes are distinct\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-block-layout.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/native-layout-project.js\u003c/code>; full lesson/current acceptance queued; no matching TMNT Audio profile is installed in this catalog\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Zelda II, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open native World for the supported overworld path; select West Hyrule and paint terrain; save/reopen/build; separately use supported location fields or an available Scene stream in its declared context\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compressed overworld terrain, packed location records and Scene streams are separate formats; moving a location does not repaint terrain or prove travel\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/zelda-overworld.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/native-world-project.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/native-engine-project.js\u003c/code>; dedicated lesson expansion/current acceptance queued\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Ninja Gaiden, investigated US revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open the supported source through the existing spatial/game recovery tools; select a recovered Scene, pose or script; inspect its current field/build capability before editing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Scene layout, metasprites, animation scripts and actor records are different native resources; recovery of each does not imply an inverse writer\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/game-re-native.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/spatial-native.js\u003c/code>; full lesson and exact editable-field inventory remain queued; keep the prior workflow accessible\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Castlevania II and Mega Man 2, investigated US revisions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect through the installed analysis/spatial and ROM Audio Library paths; inspect or audition a cue; explicitly preview any tracker conversion\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Installed analysis/Audio paths do not establish a complete native game-editing workflow\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/spatial-native.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/native-audio-catalog.js\u003c/code>; title lessons and current acceptance remain queued\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Any other ROM or unsupported revision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open ROM / project \u2192 Inspect bytes / CHR; RE / DISASM; retain independent notes, mappings and external patches\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>No decoder/writer can be assumed; a pattern sheet is not an assembled scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>General guide, sections 5\u20139; record unresolved domains instead of substituting unrelated content\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>All \u003ccode>re/\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>services/\u003c/code> paths in this table are below \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code>. Existing Audio profiles for Zelda II, Ninja Gaiden, Castlevania II, Mega Man 2, Metroid, Lolo, River City, Shadowgate and Punch-Out are revision-bound. Exact cue support and conversion limits differ. Missing Audio for a title remains an open verification item.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects-read-the-methods-inside-the-standalone-app\">Read the methods inside the standalone app\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Project lessons\u003c/strong> opens the maintained handbook chapters inside \u003cstrong>TECH DOCS\u003c/strong>. Resources and Audio have contextual lesson shortcuts; the chapter/section selectors retain independent browsing. The reader shows the active project's output scope separately from current/independent/detached source roles. Existing Tech Docs stay available below it.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The \u003ca href=\"#native-pages\">native source-page lesson\u003c/a> covers the existing block/structure workflow and title-specific boundaries. \u003ca href=\"#zelda2\">Zelda II\u003c/a> and \u003ca href=\"#ninja\">Ninja Gaiden\u003c/a> now have bounded native/source-copy lessons alongside the full \u003ca href=\"#punch-out\">Punch-Out tutorial\u003c/a>. These lessons do not claim complete inverse writers or whole-game acceptance for any title. Other title/checkpoint notes keep their documented limits; deeper format and owner verification work remains queued where shown above.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects-what-every-title-tutorial-must-deliver\">What every title tutorial must deliver\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A beginner lesson: open the exact input, find the actual scene/area, make one declared supported edit, Undo/Redo, save/reopen, rebuild, reimport and inspect it in the original engine.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A complete asset inventory with per-domain status: decoded, materialized, editable, native writer, verified context and remaining gaps. Cover graphics, palettes, placement, animation, actors, collisions, menus/text, Worlds/links, code, Audio and samples even when unsupported.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A format lesson: bank/address-to-file mapping, pointer and compression rules, palette/raster state, source aliases, byte ownership and storage limits. Use a synthetic exercise when teaching data that should not be distributed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Independent workflows: do RE and write code without Forge, patch a copied ROM in a hex editor, and inspect/reimport the result. Show a worked expected-byte patch only after its exact revision and consumers are known.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>An application lesson: the decoder \u2192 projection \u2192 ProjectStore \u2192 editor \u2192 native writer connections; exact modules/HTML markers; how to bundle a customized standalone HTML.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A separate authored-game lesson: bring supported material into a new project, create missing behavior explicitly, build a standalone ROM and test it outside Forge. State the current conversion gaps.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audio verification, original-engine checks, reproducible commands and an owner checklist. Automated checks never silently check listening, playability, controller or hardware boxes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>A title checkpoint is documentation-complete only for its declared scope when someone can reproduce its lesson from the named application and exact input. Open fields remain open. Expanding a title's scope requires updating its tutorial in the same checkpoint.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/README.md", "sourceSha256": "21a518fea7c141d8627011fd8ac821b3e215058660869ae02e163537da7a5ba9"}, {"id": "native-pages", "title": "Native source pages and structures", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"native-pages-native-source-pages-supported-edits-and-independent-workflows\">Native source pages: supported edits and independent workflows\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>This lesson covers the registered partial native page/layout adapters. The project-specific capability strip names the active adapter and its actual writers. A catalogued page is a view of a source record, not proof that the whole game, collision, actors, menus or Audio has been recovered. Use the exact supported revision; a filename does not identify it.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-pages-open-choose-a-page-and-make-one-encodable-edit\">Open, choose a page and make one encodable edit\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Save your current work. \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong> checks a chosen input without replacing the project. Review its capabilities and source SHA-256.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Prepare recovered resources (partial)\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>Prepare recovered layouts (partial)\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Import as new project\u003c/strong>. This explicit action replaces the workspace and preserves the ROM, source catalog and native recipe. Save Project As includes those privately in your project file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Area library\u003c/strong>, choose the source context, available room/table slot and page. Open a recovered page in Scene. Unavailable source slots remain unavailable; the 64 working Scene slots are not the size of the source game catalog.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In Scene, use \u003cstrong>NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS\u003c/strong>. Choose an original block and column/row, then \u003cstrong>Replace selected cell\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Paint native blocks on Scene\u003c/strong> routes canvas clicks through that same recipe. In \u003cstrong>NATIVE ROOM STRUCTURES\u003c/strong>, choose an existing object and replacement structure, then \u003cstrong>Replace selected structure\u003c/strong>. This does not add/move native objects.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect the page and any shared uses. Undo and Redo. Open \u003cstrong>Connected Workspace \u2192 Project Health \u2192 Check current ROM build\u003c/strong> to run the actual encoder/compiler. Source allocation, protected cells, visibility and shared-record constraints can refuse an edit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>, reopen in a fresh app and build again. Compare output bytes, reimport the exported ROM, and inspect the same source record. Finally reach it in the original engine and an independent emulator. Recorded checks do not replace your gameplay/listening/controller/hardware pass.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-pages-methods-differ-by-title\">Methods differ by title\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Installed adapter\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Source and editing method\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Boundary to inspect\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Contra\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compressed 32\u00d732 blocks and row-copy commands; explicit initial CHR-RAM loads\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Alternate graphics contexts can refuse. A source record is not a universal scene or actor encoder.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Final Fantasy\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compressed 64\u00d764 standard maps; existing 16\u00d716 block substitutions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Normal/room palette views share map data; page edges can split blocks. This is not a native overworld writer.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Mega Man\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Column-major room storage with shared pointer slots; existing 32\u00d732 blocks\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A 256-pixel room spans view boundaries. Initial graphics do not cover every transition. Mega Man 2 Audio is a different source/profile.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>TMNT\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bounded Area 1 side-view screen table; existing 32\u00d732 block substitutions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Other areas, object behavior and collision semantics need their own recovery.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Metroid\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Ordered room objects and whole-structure substitutions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Positions/palettes and overlap effects remain native. Use its dedicated room and Audio lessons.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Adventures of Lolo\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bottom-up compressed terrain rows in CHR-backed storage\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Only allowed interior cells/codes; actor/settings cells and per-room capacity are protected.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>River City Ransom\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing tile substitutions in a recovered street-background crop\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Physical row bytes can be shared. Interiors/special screens have a separate unresolved path.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Shadowgate\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing tile substitutions in a 112\u00d7112 picture crop\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Objects, doors, alternate states and puzzle behavior are separate resources.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Punch-Out\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native PPU staging records plus dedicated arena, physical artwork and Glass Joe position writers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A staging page or pose-art copy is not the dedicated writer. Use the complete Punch-Out lesson for those controls.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>The table describes installed methods, not fresh whole-game acceptance for every title. Follow the current encoder message and source evidence. Unknown revisions retain independent analysis and preserved-source workflows; they do not inherit another title's writer.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-pages-graphics-audio-and-other-assets\">Graphics, Audio and other assets\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>CHR pixels, palette colors, palette IDs, Scene layout, Scene OAM, Metasprite pieces, collision properties and native recipes are distinct owners. A displayed buffer can be a protected reference copy. Check \u003cstrong>Resources\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>Scene resources\u003c/strong>, and \u003cstrong>Native tools\u003c/strong> before painting generic pixels. A supported block/structure substitution does not grant arbitrary pixel, palette, object, animation or runtime-rule writeback.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Audio is project-wide unless explicit source-event evidence exists. \u003cstrong>ROM Audio Library\u003c/strong> identifies a separate revision-bound catalog, offers supported isolated native audition, and can preview an editable finite tracker transcription. Import is a separate action. Unsupported profiles, PCM/DPCM or timing limits stay visible. Transcription into tracker assets does not provide native Audio writeback.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Project intake labels its last input separately from the current project source. A detached catalog is retained evidence with stale actions disabled. \u003cstrong>Continue independent Audio inspection\u003c/strong> explicitly reopens a retained ROM through the existing source loader without replacing your project or emulator. An independent source can be useful for another project; its hash/name and scope must stay visible.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"native-pages-independent-re-code-and-hex-work\">Independent RE, code and hex work\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Keep the source ROM and native JSON backup. Use \u003cstrong>RE / DISASM\u003c/strong> to inspect physical offsets, declared CPU mappings and original bytes; disassembly candidates are not a complete source project. Export your annotations. A standalone \u003cstrong>6502 Source Workspace\u003c/strong> retains multi-file code and \u003ccode>.include\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>.incbin\u003c/code> data separately and builds only its declared Forge NROM profile; it does not translate arbitrary game engines.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To patch externally, work on a copy, identify the exact source revision, source bank/offset, command/compression rules and shared consumers, then compare the changed byte range. Reimport the derivative only through a method that explicitly recognizes it. Do not turn a wider recognition mask into write permission, flatten raster states, or overwrite a protected signature to force a build.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For application changes, locate \u003ccode>services/native-resource-project.js\u003c/code>, the title's \u003ccode>re/native-*-resources.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/native-layout-project.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/native-layout-ui.js\u003c/code>, ProjectStore and BuildGraph comments. Edit modular \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, run the existing bundle/root synchronization commands, and regenerate the optional hosted output from the same source. An HTML-only experiment can search those module comments in a copied standalone file. Changing the application HTML is distinct from editing game assets.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The developer/asset guide explains these paths in full. Each expanded title still needs its own precise format, consumers, byte ownership, Audio and original-engine acceptance lesson as new methods are recovered.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/NATIVE_SOURCE_PAGES.md", "sourceSha256": "0ae7686bad5c3773f0021ab11e669c158ef1b81c5fdd65b2857ee7b3fe967680"}, {"id": "zelda2", "title": "Zelda II native World and location workflow", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"zelda2-zelda-ii-native-terrain-location-fields-and-source-context\">Zelda II: native terrain, location fields and source context\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>This lesson describes the existing bounded editor workflows. Hosted multiplayer source-room identity, transitions and combat have their own versioned contracts; multiplayer behavior is not certified by editing an overworld map.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"zelda2-edit-the-supported-native-overworld\">Edit the supported native overworld\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Save your current project, then choose \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong> and the investigated Zelda II revision. Review source identity, capabilities and limits. Choose \u003cstrong>Open editable native overworld\u003c/strong>. The separate native World entry uses the same terrain owner.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select \u003cstrong>West Hyrule\u003c/strong> in the native World selector. The selected terrain becomes a canonical World with one shared terrain tileset. East Hyrule, Death Mountain and Maze Island use their own source records and compressed budgets; open them explicitly when needed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pick a native terrain brush and paint a small supported change. Undo and Redo. The terrain encoder checks the original compressed allocation before accepting a stroke; simplify runs if it refuses. Native terrain behavior stays in the source engine.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>, reopen in a fresh app and select the same World. \u003cstrong>Check current ROM build\u003c/strong> verifies current eligibility. \u003cstrong>Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong> runs the edited native engine; reach the changed terrain, then inspect the exported ROM in another emulator. A selected editor World does not select the runtime start/spawn of an authored World project.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"zelda2-location-fields-and-recovered-references\">Location fields and recovered references\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Connected Workspace \u2192 Native tools\u003c/strong> lists supported location controls. The verified location writer covers X/Y, scene selector and entry code only. Moving a location does not repaint its terrain or icon, establish a safe/reachable entrance, or recover quest progression. Follow the original source table and its packed flags; unsupported contexts must refuse.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Project investigation\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>Area library\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Resources\u003c/strong> expose source evidence, reference art, copied poses and ordinary project assets. Native source bindings keep their original owner. Generic art, actor, collision, Audio and runtime edits without a registered encoder remain savable but can block native output. A reference-room World is not the whole overworld.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>ROM Audio Library\u003c/strong> for a matching source catalog, native audition and explicit finite tracker conversion where supported. Scene-event evidence is not a new native song/trigger writer. Source cue inspection and authored Audio remain distinct. Inspect source/current/detached labels when opening another file or project.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"zelda2-work-with-code-hex-or-html\">Work with code, hex or HTML\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Use independent \u003cstrong>RE / DISASM\u003c/strong> for physical source bytes, CPU-bank interpretation and annotations. Source actor/animation/collision evidence is incomplete and must not be upgraded into runtime claims. Keep a ROM backup and saved native project. External patches need exact bank/offset, packed-field masks, original compressed-map capacity and a verified derivative-recognition path; never edit the projection hash to bypass a refusal.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The terrain path connects \u003ccode>re/zelda-overworld.js\u003c/code> \u2192 \u003ccode>services/native-world-project.js\u003c/code> \u2192 canonical World \u2192 \u003ccode>world/native-world-ui.js\u003c/code> \u2192 the existing BuildGraph/native backend. Location records use \u003ccode>re/native-engine-format.js\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>services/native-engine-project.js\u003c/code>. ProjectStore owns persistence/Undo; reference navigation uses SceneContext/AreaLibrary. None is a second World or source store.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a copied standalone HTML experiment, search those module comments or \u003ccode>nativeWorldOpen\u003c/code>. For maintained changes, edit \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, regenerate the native/root and optional hosted outputs, and run native terrain/save/reopen regressions. The comprehensive developer guide and original Tech Docs cover independent 6502 assembly, source exports, CHR, actor and authored-game workflows. Original characters, spells, items and native stats must not be replaced with invented rules merely to connect an editor.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/ZELDA_II.md", "sourceSha256": "a39fba4833c7c9c45a7b60b586048ea17fa2bea3fefa32eabd2693fcc2b4db51"}, {"id": "ninja", "title": "Ninja Gaiden source and editable copies", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"ninja-ninja-gaiden-investigation-editable-copies-and-preserved-source\">Ninja Gaiden: investigation, editable copies and preserved source\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Choose the exact investigated revision. Decoded rooms, sprite poses, native action scripts, actor records, CHR and Audio are different resource families. Their presence in a catalog does not establish an inverse encoder for the complete game.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"ninja-open-the-investigation-and-choose-the-intended-output\">Open the investigation and choose the intended output\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Save current work. Use \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong>, review the source SHA-256/capability report, then \u003cstrong>Prepare investigated project \u2192 Import as new project\u003c/strong>. The saved project retains its original ROM and recovered catalogs. A fresh reopen restores source access.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Project investigation\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>Area library\u003c/strong> and choose the source area, room and page. \u003cstrong>Scene resources\u003c/strong> aligns the matching editable context without cloning assets or changing unrelated source ownership. Stage/table membership is evidence; it does not prove runtime reachability or route order.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For original-engine output, inspect \u003cstrong>Native tools\u003c/strong>. The preserved native Ninja workflow has unchanged-source output; generic Scene/CHR/pose/actor/Audio changes have no verified native writer here. Unsupported work stays savable and the native backend refuses it. Undo such edits to return to unchanged output.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For an authored Forge adaptation, use the existing selected-resource conversion/export paths to obtain editable copies or a standalone ordinary project. Review conversion limits, source Scene references, unassigned collision, untimed or partial data and capacity. Author missing gameplay explicitly, check the generated backend, then build/test that adaptation. It is a new authored runtime with source provenance, not a complete rebuild of Ninja's engine.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In either workflow, Save Project As, reopen in a fresh app and compare intended assets/output. Use explicit \u003cstrong>Run Original\u003c/strong> only when you intend to play preserved source rather than current compiled edits.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"ninja-poses-scripts-audio-and-manual-investigation\">Poses, scripts, Audio and manual investigation\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Metasprite pieces keep their source Scene's pixels/palette; Animation references preserve explicit frame IDs. A script preview is recovered timing evidence within its declared initial state, not complete enemy AI, combat or collision. Resources shows declared connections and snapshots without fabricating Scene OAM or song bindings.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>ROM Audio Library\u003c/strong> uses revision-bound cue/driver methods. Inspect a cue, audition supported native execution, and preview an editable finite score before importing into tracker assets. Source begin/reset sound requests are evidence; they do not create a new native trigger or Audio writer. A detached project catalog disables stale actions, while an explicitly chosen independent source remains available for read-only investigation and deliberate conversion.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For independent RE or external hex editing, retain the original ROM, inspect bank/physical offsets in \u003cstrong>RE / DISASM\u003c/strong>, export annotations, and record exact changed ranges and consumers. Do not assume a pose/script pointer is a writable decoded frame or that a familiar instruction stream establishes full source recovery. Original-source identity and native projection checks remain enforced.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For independent coding, \u003cstrong>Code \u2192 Open source workspace\u003c/strong> preserves multi-file source and builds its declared NROM profile separately; export that workspace before closing. It does not translate Ninja's native build/toolchain. The general developer guide covers assembler, byte/bitplane, source mapping and external patch workflows.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For application changes, inspect \u003ccode>re/game-re-native.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/spatial-native.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/game-re-assets.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/investigation-project.js\u003c/code>, ProjectStore and the native backend. In a copied HTML, search their module comments. Maintain changes in \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, keep shared native/hosted manifests, regenerate both outputs and verify the preserved-source and authored-copy paths separately.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/NINJA_GAIDEN.md", "sourceSha256": "4aa2619c9599329d5dd8f86105a7dff77c8dd5703748ecdccc7d1ed9d49ef96a"}, {"id": "punch-out", "title": "Punch-Out worked tutorial", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"punch-out-punch-out-import-edit-rebuild-and-understand-the-method\">Punch-Out: import, edit, rebuild and understand the method\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>This lesson covers the current Mike Tyson's Punch-Out!! US PRG0/PRG1 support. You will make a supported arena edit, preserve it in a project, build a separate ROM, inspect fighter/player/referee references, audition source Audio and find the code needed to extend the method. The bounded original-engine replay, reversible knockdown/round experiments, physical CHR/fight-palette writers and Glass Joe full/explicit-incremental position writer extend that workflow. Complete fights, individual native pose geometry, native animation and Audio writers remain open.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The combined review application is \u003ccode>NES_Forge_Retro_Punch_Out_Native_Workflows.html\u003c/code>, identical to \u003ccode>app/mainline/NES_Forge_2.0.0-alpha.11.html\u003c/code>. Its SHA-256 is \u003ccode>cf5825b600fc8f8c028e9df0def70cc1cac483408ad49b8327715b02a4598e08\u003c/code>. Sections 13\u201315 describe the new dedicated artwork, original-engine replay and Retro Workstation controls. The preceding verified fight-reference checkpoint remains separately available at local commit \u003ccode>e2d94cb\u003c/code> (app SHA-256 \u003ccode>aceb8cdd70e0839b4649bc56daa39519204b660bbbae100209ad9e66bb03b89a\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-1-keep-the-right-files\">1. Keep the right files\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Keep four distinct files: the application HTML, your original ROM, your saved Forge project JSON and your rebuilt \u003ccode>.nes\u003c/code>. The project preserves the source and supported edits; the application supplies the tools. Later application versions can open saved projects, but unsaved browser state does not move between HTML files.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Investigated input\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth style=\"text-align:right;\">Bytes\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>SHA-256\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>US PRG0\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd style=\"text-align:right;\">262160\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>0435f6d9bcfeb474a592c3a950530a99c03a6f6cacff793dc88f822bb8cc6952\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>US PRG1\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd style=\"text-align:right;\">262160\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>22ce21aa9c477d87e9cdc386b185f2166b18d438257640aa1d8e75ee9960e06e\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>The adapter also admits bounded derivatives in its declared native edit ranges after validating their streams and protected identity. Other regions, revisions and arbitrary hacks are not established by this lesson. Use your own source; no ROM or extracted game assets are included here.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-2-first-lesson-change-the-shared-arena\">2. First lesson: change the shared arena\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open the current application in a browser. Save any current work. Choose \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong>, choose your supported original, \u003cstrong>Prepare recovered resources (partial)\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Import as new project\u003c/strong>. If you already have a saved recovered project, open that JSON instead.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Recovered content \u2192 Open ring and audience\u003c/strong>. Select \u003cstrong>Arena \u00b7 ring and audience \u00b7 source setup\u003c/strong>. This is assembled placement, not a raw pattern sheet. Fighter-source Scenes can intentionally have blank backgrounds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Before editing, use \u003cstrong>Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong> and retain a baseline output. An unchanged native build must preserve the input exactly. The original game starts; the source Scene is an editing view, not a replacement reset screen.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In the arena Scene, choose an existing tile and change one ordinary ring/audience cell using the Scene tools. Use a small change that is easy to recognize. Record its zero-based column/row and original/new native tile IDs. Avoid \u003ccode>$FD\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>$FE\u003c/code> in the beginner exercise: pattern reads of those tiles control graphics-bank latches. Keep the other asset fields unchanged.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test \u003cstrong>Undo\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Redo\u003c/strong>. Use \u003cstrong>Check arena build\u003c/strong>. The writer supports layout tile IDs and visible attribute palette IDs. Changing an attribute chooses a palette group; it does not change the four actual NES color values.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use \u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>. Close/reopen the JSON in a fresh application instance and confirm the intended arena edit. Use \u003cstrong>Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong> and download the built ROM through the existing build controls.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enter a fight in the original game and inspect the shared arena. HUD/player updates can overwrite cells; choose a stable ring/audience location and inspect multiple rows. Check that Audio remains present. External emulator play is a separate useful acceptance check.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reopen the rebuilt ROM through \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong> and prepare/import again. Supported arena derivatives should retain recognition, the arena edit, recovered source content and the Audio catalog. Preserve the project JSON as the richer editing record.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>If the writer reports a capacity failure, simplify the pattern or Undo. Nothing should be silently dropped. A successful native build does not prove the edit preserves a playable fight or every later state.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-3-know-which-assets-you-are-editing\">3. Know which assets you are editing\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Domain\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Current recovery/editor\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Native output and limits\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Five staging backgrounds\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original PPU programs, placement, attributes and latch-aware background rows; source pages in Scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Dedicated native brush supports declared literal substitutions; generated/inherited and protected aliases refuse\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Ring/audience arena\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Two original setup nametables projected across the source scroll seam; ordinary Scene layout\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Arena writer supports \u003ccode>nt\u003c/code> and visible attribute bits within fixed capacity; shared native arena affects its game uses\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Background and sprite CHR\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>32 physical 4 KiB banks; source banks and projected references available\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open a dedicated physical CHR view for native pixel writing; edits to copied reference art still have no reference writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Palette colors\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source palettes retained for each declared context\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Dedicated native fight-palette views now write color bits while preserving flags; reference copies remain protected\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Opponents\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>14 fight configurations, source pose records, separate sprite CHR/OAM and explicit Metasprite materialization\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Dedicated Glass Joe views write 30 full and five explicit incremental X/Y records; the idle set can move together, with native priority derived from Y; copied reference poses remain snapshots; individual geometry, tiles, flags and other opponents need separate pose writers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Opponent animations\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bounded original state execution retains RAM/OAM, incremental poses and size changes; explicit Animation import\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Observed excerpts have declared duration/initial state; no complete AI, fight timing or native animation writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Little Mac\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>98 bank-11 background command descriptors; bounded player-state paths\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Commands can clear cells or draw fragments; not all are complete poses; native command/art writing absent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Referee/count/speech\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>47 extended bank-11 command descriptors; available player/referee state paths\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Same background system, not opponent metasprites; no native writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>HUD/scroll/raster\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original setup HUD routines plus declared bank/scroll context in fight references; canonical raster representation\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reference composition is savable; it is not a complete live HUD or captured fight; reference art edits block native building\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Actors/AI/damage/collision\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Pointer/state evidence and limited source observations\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Complete concurrent behavior, hit/damage rules and editable native bindings remain unresolved\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Menus/transitions/game phases\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Five declared staging contexts plus separate source evidence\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Complete menu graph, round controller, entrances and transitions remain unresolved\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Worlds/entities/links\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>No complete boxing-game World or Forge gameplay conversion is supplied\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Creating authored records does not patch original game behavior\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Music/SFX\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original driver/catalog, native audition, finite explicit tracker adaptation\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>No native Punch-Out Audio writer; adapted tracker changes save but block native output\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>DPCM/startup PCM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native sample/handler evidence and isolated audition\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>No generic sample tracker conversion or native Audio sample writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Code/RAM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>RE/disassembly plus source pointers and bounded interpreter evidence\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Code and RAM experiments need their own independent patch/rebuild workflow; not automatic native code editing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>For the field-by-field canonical asset map and JavaScript transaction example, read the general developer guide, sections 3 and 13. A reference copy is an asset, but it is not the authority for writing every byte represented in the picture.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-4-staging-backgrounds-and-source-aliases\">4. Staging backgrounds and source aliases\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>Area library \u2192 Investigated source\u003c/strong> to select an available staging context/page. The native block controls project a narrow physical-literal recipe. Choose the native block ID and cell through \u003cstrong>NATIVE SCREEN BLOCKS\u003c/strong> or its canvas brush. Ordinary unrestricted Scene painting is not a substitute for a recipe-owned staging edit.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>These five contexts come from declared combinations of source programs; 84 available command programs are decoded out of 95 table slots. There are 711 writable bytes in 97 spans per revision. A repeat operand can supply many displayed cells, and several contexts can share it. The native owner updates those aliases together. Refusal for inherited/generated cells or metadata aliases protects source semantics.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Repeat the same Undo/Redo \u2192 save/reopen \u2192 build \u2192 original-engine \u2192 derivative-reimport exercise. Check the other affected contexts. Do not interpret the five staging views as five complete fights or all game menus.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-5-fighter-poses-and-observed-opponent-animation\">5. Fighter poses and observed opponent animation\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong>. Choose \u003cstrong>Fighter / fight\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Pose\u003c/strong>. Inspect the source bank, palette offsets and pointer evidence. The preview is centered for inspection; that centering does not change native placement and does not simulate hardware scanline clipping.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Open pose in Metasprites\u003c/strong> to materialize that supported source pose, or \u003cstrong>Recover background pages and fighter samples\u003c/strong> for the bounded overview. It uses existing Scenes/metasprites and one Undo transaction. It does not replace the source ROM.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expand \u003cstrong>State animation excerpts\u003c/strong>. Select a source state and a bounded \u003cstrong>Native updates\u003c/strong> window, then \u003cstrong>Trace state\u003c/strong>. Inspect observed frames, duration and sprite-size changes. Some entries require context and can refuse an isolated pose while a valid preceding trace establishes it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Open recovered animation\u003c/strong> to add the canonical excerpt. Save/reopen and inspect Animation/Metasprites. \u003cstrong>Inspect linked sound\u003c/strong> opens observed source Audio requests; those requests are not automatically authored runtime events.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Save animation/art experiments separately. Editing them is useful, but native rebuilding requires undoing unsupported changes or returning to a saved native-buildable baseline. Reopening a copied animation does not create a missing native writer.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>Browse all CHR banks\u003c/strong> for physical patterns. Tile patterns, assembled poses and timed state execution answer different questions. The game changes sprite size and CHR context, so decoding each shape as an independent static picture can produce the wrong result.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-6-mac-referee-and-hud-composition\">6. Mac, referee and HUD composition\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>In \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong>, expand \u003cstrong>Glass Joe fight reference \u00b7 Little Mac / referee / HUD\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Start with Mac command 0, referee command 144 and Glass Joe source pose 0; choose \u003cstrong>Preview pose composition\u003c/strong>. It uses original setup health/hearts/stars/score/clock routines. The opponent is at the original configuration's upper-right opening position; this is a declared setup reference.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Change a \u003cstrong>Little Mac command\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>Referee / speech command\u003c/strong>, then preview again. Commands can update only a fragment, clear cells or change speech/count data. Do not expect every entry to be a full self-contained character.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Native player state\u003c/strong>, set \u003cstrong>Native updates\u003c/strong> from 1 to 600 and choose \u003cstrong>Trace player / referee state\u003c/strong>. Select an \u003cstrong>Observed excerpt frame\u003c/strong>. The 23 available entries trace a declared zero-input path. \u003ccode>update-limit\u003c/code> means observation stopped at the budget; other game systems or input may be needed to progress.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Open selected fight reference\u003c/strong>. This adds an ordinary Scene with separate opponent OAM and bank/scroll/raster provenance in one Undo transaction. Save/reopen it. It is labeled as a recovered source reference, not verified runtime capture.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Edit native arena layout\u003c/strong> to return to the actual arena write owner. Paint there, then preview again: the composition uses the current encoded arena when native building succeeds. If unsupported work blocks building, inspection can explicitly fall back to preserved source plus existing literal recipes; read its warning.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>Editing the reference's own CHR, palettes, sprites or background remains savable and blocks native building. Such work cannot be silently re-signed as unchanged source. Preview guards also reject materialization when the project changed after preview; preview again after a legitimate edit.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The trace updates source state then uploads its command immediately. It does not reproduce original NMI arbitration, concurrent opponent AI, damage or complete fight phases. Fine scroll and MMC2 reads remain modeled rather than flattened into an ordinary tile grid.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-7-audio-including-the-startup-sample\">7. Audio, including the startup sample\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Open source Audio\u003c/strong>, or \u003cstrong>AUDIO \u2192 ROM Audio Library\u003c/strong> after import/reopen. Check that the source profile matches your revision. There are 84 entries, including six empty slots and two stop controls; a catalog entry is not always a sounding cue.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select music or an effect; choose \u003cstrong>Inspect cue\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Play native\u003c/strong>. Stop playback before another experiment. The bank-8 driver supplies 23 sounding music selections, 45 procedural effects and seven DPCM selections.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select \u003cstrong>Startup PCM + native bell\u003c/strong> and use at least 199 native updates for the full isolated routine. Shorter windows report truncation. This separate bank-6 routine produces direct DAC writes; it is not ordinary tracker music.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For representable music/effects, choose \u003cstrong>Preview editable score\u003c/strong>, review the declared losses, then explicitly import into the tracker. Check Undo/Redo and save/reopen. A music import changes the canonical order/tempo; preserve a separate experiment if you want to continue native arena building.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo an unsupported Audio import before the next native build. Reopen the arena derivative and check its native Audio catalog/audition again. Source sample bytes and original driver code should remain preserved.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>The native machine executes original timing/driver requests in a repeatable isolated context. Tracker conversion is finite and frame-sampled; DPCM and short-mode noise can be omitted or conversion refused. It does not recover complete song loop structure, native instruments or game event routing. Automated APU/PCM checks do not establish browser listening quality.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-8-how-this-particular-format-works\">8. How this particular format works\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>This investigated iNES layout has a 16-byte header, 128 KiB PRG and 128 KiB CHR. MMC2 selects PRG/CHR banks; a CPU address is not a unique file offset. For a declared 8 KiB PRG bank mapped at \u003ccode>$8000\u2013$9FFF\u003c/code>, the physical offset is \u003ccode>16 + bank * 8192 + (cpuAddress - $8000)\u003c/code>. Fixed windows need their actual selected physical bank too.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The arena writer owns four two-byte pointers in physical bank 12 at CPU addresses \u003ccode>$917B\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$917E\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$9184\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$9187\u003c/code>, plus 381 bytes at \u003ccode>$9190\u2013$930C\u003c/code>. Their file offsets are respectively \u003ccode>$1918B\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$1918E\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$19194\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$19197\u003c/code>, and the storage range is \u003ccode>$191A0\u2013$1931C\u003c/code>. End addresses here are inclusive. These are exact-source arena ownership ranges, not general free space.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The writer starts from original raw nametables, maps visible cells across the scroll seam through \u003ccode>cellSources\u003c/code>, updates visible attributes, compresses each page into at most two source streams, and updates the owned pointers. It refuses total-capacity or per-stream overflow and decodes the candidate with the original loader. Offscreen cells and unrelated code/CHR/Audio stay protected.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The native literal brush instead owns individual source operands. Its writer is not interchangeable with the arena compressor. Both flow through \u003ccode>NativeResourceProject\u003c/code> and the existing native backend.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>MMC2 switches CHR during pattern-address reads. A tile outside the visible viewport can select the bank for a subsequent row. Rendering retains 34 background fetches per scanline; clipping pixels cannot remove those reads. Mac and referee use bank-11 nametable commands, while opponent poses use OAM/shape routines. These differences explain the separate recovery modules.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-9-independent-re-code-and-hex-editing\">9. Independent RE, code and hex editing\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>You can investigate without using the recovery buttons. Hash a copied original; record its bank map; disassemble a declared bank at its CPU origin; follow native pointer consumers; record inputs, RAM changes, PPU/OAM writes and sound requests. Keep notes about starting state and stop conditions. General analysis is read-only; reversible RAM experiments affect an emulator session rather than the ROM.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>As a read-only source exercise, run Node from the repository root. Save the following as \u003ccode>inspect-punch-out.cjs\u003c/code> there and pass your own original path. It writes only summaries to the terminal:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>const fs = require('node:fs');\nconst crypto = require('node:crypto');\nconst bytes = new Uint8Array(fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2]));\nconst hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(bytes).digest('hex');\nconst formats = require('./app/src/re/native-punch-out-resources.js');\nconst profile = formats.profiles.find(p =&gt; p.kind === 'punch-out' &amp;&amp; p.sha256 === hash);\nif (!profile) throw Error('Use an investigated original for this exercise.');\nconst content = require('./app/src/re/punch-out-content.js').open(bytes);\nconst arena = require('./app/src/re/punch-out-arena.js').open(bytes);\nconst fight = require('./app/src/re/punch-out-fight.js').open(bytes);\nconsole.log({profile: profile.id, fights: content.fights.length,\n  arenaCells: arena.nt.length, commands: fight.poses.length,\n  availablePlayerStates: fight.states.filter(s =&gt; s.available).length});\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node inspect-punch-out.cjs /path/to/original.nes\nnode app/docs/native-codec-exercise.cjs\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>Expected current counts are 14 fight configurations, 960 arena cells, 145 combined Mac/referee command descriptors and 23 available state entries. The second command teaches decode \u2192 edit \u2192 encode \u2192 reopen \u2192 reimport using a deliberately synthetic format. Its bytes are not Punch-Out command bytes.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For external hex work, use the mapped offsets above to inspect arena pointers and compare an app-built candidate with the exact original. Verify every changed byte falls within the declared owners for the edit you made. Those compressed bytes do not have one visual-cell-to-byte correspondence; a layout edit can change stream lengths and pointer values. Do not type a tile number into a guessed compressed offset.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For your own supported arena encoder, follow \u003ccode>PunchOutArenaEncoder.encode(bytes, {nt, attr})\u003c/code> and its \u003ccode>spans\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>capacity\u003c/code> contract on a copy, then validate with the independent original-loader checks. The service adds project-signature, alias and unsupported-field checks beyond this low-level helper. Using the helper alone is not complete project build validation.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a code patch, identify its callers, native bank, CPU origin, owned space, vectors, stack/RAM effects and timing; assemble externally or through the supported assembler into a separate patched ROM. Verify exact expected original bytes and all changed ranges. CODE text in a preserved native project is not automatically injected. Changes outside admitted native ranges can lose specialized recognition and still be inspectable through general RE.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-10-modify-the-html-and-understand-the-connections\">10. Modify the HTML and understand the connections\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Task\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Owner below \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Connection\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Detect revision and decode staging programs\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-punch-out-resources.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-layout.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source catalog \u2192 native resource recipe/Scene projection\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Decode opponents and bounded state paths\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-content.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-states.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Shared CPU core/private bus \u2192 source pose/state evidence\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Materialize opponents/animations\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-content-recovery.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/punch-out-state-recovery.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Canonical Scenes/metasprites/animations \u2192 ProjectStore\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Decode and project arena\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-arena.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/punch-out-arena-recovery.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original setup programs \u2192 ordinary arena Scene\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Encode arena\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-arena-encoder.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/punch-out-arena-writer.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Registered writer \u2192 NativeResourceProject \u2192 BuildGraph/RuntimeCompiler\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Compose Mac/referee/HUD\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-fight.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Private source execution \u2192 canonical raster/CHR/OAM projection\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Preserve source references\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-fight-recovery.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prior-build validation \u2192 detached projection \u2192 guarded canonical addition\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Connect buttons\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-content-ui.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-states-ui.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-arena-ui.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-fight-ui.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Preview \u2192 epoch/serialization guard \u2192 snapshot \u2192 ProjectStore commit \u2192 existing editor\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native Audio\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-audio.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/native-audio-machine.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>audio/native-audio-library-ui.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source driver/catalog \u2192 isolated native execution \u2192 explicit adaptation\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Mapper/player\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/mmc2.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/mmc2-emulator.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>PRG/CHR/mirroring/latch state \u2192 existing emulator; live capture remains unsupported\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>For a small worked app change, copy the HTML, search \u003ccode>NES FORGE MODULE: src/re/punch-out-fight-ui.js\u003c/code>, then locate the help paragraph that starts \u003ccode>Source-defined opening placement\u003c/code>. Change its explanatory wording without changing source evidence/signatures. Reopen the copy and check startup, Recovered content, an old saved project and Audio. This changes application help for future sessions, not game data.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For the maintained source version, make the same change in \u003ccode>app/src/re/punch-out-fight-ui.js\u003c/code>, then run:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python3 app/tools/bundle.py\npython3 app/tools/bundle.py --check\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>app/source-manifest.json\u003c/code> controls dependency order. The output is a complete standalone HTML; the bundler does not fetch game assets or require a server. Direct-only HTML edits disappear when you regenerate it. JavaScript inside each module has its own scope; use documented service boundaries rather than a second project store.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-11-build-a-separate-standalone-game\">11. Build a separate standalone game\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>There are three distinct outcomes. The preceding native lesson produces a standalone \u003ccode>.nes\u003c/code> using the preserved original engine. The HTML lesson produces a customized standalone NES Forge application. Creating a new original game is an authored-project workflow with its own behavior and build backend.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For that new game, start a separate authored project and save it immediately. Create original or otherwise usable art; explicitly import only supported asset types through existing tools, or recreate a small asset with the editors. Select the appropriate Scene/CHR/palettes, assemble metasprites/animation, add the supported actor/behavior or custom assembly, then author collision, state transitions, Audio and persistence deliberately. Preserve referenced IDs and test the chosen runtime's compiled subset.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Recovered Mac/referee raster commands do not currently convert into a complete playable authored boxing actor. No one-click whole-Punch-Out conversion exists. Do not delete the native descriptor from a recovered JSON and assume all source relationships become authored behavior. Keep the native source project as a reference and maintain the authored project separately.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Build the authored ROM, save/reopen the project and test the ROM in another emulator. Check reset, input, animation, collisions, Audio and transitions. Browser controls, physical controllers and NES hardware require their own acceptance. The app HTML and the authored ROM remain separate deliverables.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-12-reproduce-checks-and-diagnose-problems\">12. Reproduce checks and diagnose problems\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>From the repository root:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python3 app/tools/bundle.py --check\nnode app/docs/native-codec-exercise.cjs\npython3 reviews/punch-out-fight-context-1/run_checks.py\nnode reviews/punch-out-fight-context-1/verify_fight.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-fight-context-1/verify_lifecycle.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-developer-1/verify_reimport.cjs /path/to/private-roms\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>The supplied-input scripts use the investigated original filenames; read the script arguments before running against another directory. No original is bundled. \u003ccode>reviews/punch-out-fight-context-1/\u003c/code> records the application identity and scope: both revisions, 145 native command comparisons, 2,224 observed state updates and seven representative full composite comparisons per revision; lifecycle checks retain 84 Audio entries. The available public gate passed 104 checks. These are checkpoint results, not a claim that this documentation reruns every historical release test or verifies a complete fight.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Inspect and recover\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Blank background beside a boxer\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Select the actual arena or explicit fight reference; the fighter source Scene is a separate sprite resource\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Arena compression exceeds capacity\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reduce layout complexity or Undo; preserve the experiment in a separate JSON\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Save works but native build refuses\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Read the unsupported-field diagnostic; restore the native baseline or implement the missing writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Pose is unavailable/context-required\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Use a valid preceding state path; do not invent missing CHR/OAM context\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Reference preview is stale\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Preview again after the project change, then materialize\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Fight reference shows old arena\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Read the build/fallback warning and check the actual arena owner; reference edits do not update it\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Latch rows look wrong\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compare untouched source too; inspect offscreen pattern reads, banks and scrolling before blaming compression\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Rebuilt output loses recognition\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Compare protected ranges and exact stream validity; unrelated patches need separate support\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Audio tracker import blocks build\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Save the adaptation separately and Undo it in the native project; no Punch-Out Audio writer is installed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Startup sample stops early\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Use at least 199 updates; inspect truncation and stop state\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-learner-acceptance\">Learner acceptance\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] I can identify my source revision, app version and separate output files.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I can reproduce one arena edit, Undo/Redo it, save/reopen, rebuild and reimport it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I inspected that edit during an actual fight and checked its shared uses and Audio.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I can distinguish raw CHR, fighter samples, opponent animation and Mac/referee background references.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I can audition music, an effect and startup PCM; I understand tracker adaptation and native writer limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I can locate the format, recovery, UI and writer owners in source or HTML.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I can inspect my own input independently and map a declared bank address without confusing it with a file offset.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I know which unsupported experiments are safely saved and how to return to a native-buildable baseline.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] I understand the separate steps and missing conversion work for a new authored game.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>These boxes require the learner's observations. Headless command/lifecycle checks do not check browser layout, listening, controls, complete fights or hardware for you.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-13-edit-native-artwork-and-fight-colors\">13. Edit native artwork and fight colors\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>Native artwork \u00b7 CHR banks and fight palettes\u003c/strong> section under \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong> is the native write path. Existing recovered poses, arena and fight-reference Scenes retain their original snapshot ownership. Editing a copy does not automatically identify a unique physical source byte.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Save a native-buildable baseline. Open \u003cstrong>Recovered content \u2192 Native artwork \u00b7 CHR banks and fight palettes\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a physical CHR bank (0\u201331), then \u003cstrong>Edit physical CHR bank\u003c/strong>. This adds/selects an ordinary, explicitly named Scene and opens \u003cstrong>CHR SHEET\u003c/strong>. Its 256 patterns are a source-bank editing view, not an assembled game room. Both background and sprite art live in physical banks; use the \u003cstrong>background CHR\u003c/strong> buffer of this dedicated bank view to edit its native pixels.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select a tile and edit a pixel using the normal CHR controls. Undo/Redo, Save Project As and reopen. The dedicated view owns exactly that 4 KiB bank's bitplanes. Its display palette is for inspection; change actual game colors through the next dedicated view.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a fight palette from the same section, then \u003cstrong>Edit native fight palette\u003c/strong>. This selects a dedicated color-table Scene. Use the background/sprite palette controls for the corresponding halves. Pixel edits on this palette-only Scene remain unsupported. Universal slot-zero colors are linked across both halves.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Check native artwork build\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong>. Download the output and reimport it. The derived ROM should retain recognition, edited physical art/colors and its original Audio driver. Native arena layout edits compose with the dedicated art writes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open a new fight reference or run the native replay to inspect the current encoded output. Earlier reference Scenes retain their source snapshots. This distinction prevents a refreshed view from silently overwriting locally edited reference material.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>The writer preserves original high palette flag bits and changes only the six NES color bits. For example, changing the displayed color of an original \u003ccode>$8F\u003c/code> entry retains its \u003ccode>$80\u003c/code> flags. Re-import identity also protects those upper bits and all unrelated code/pointers/Audio. Palette slots overridden by native setup/fade/game routines can look different in particular phases; the Glass Joe setup deliberately overrides slot 13. A color-table edit does not add new palettes, change palette selection rules or remove those source routines.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Physical CHR art is shared by every game use of that bank/tile. This writer changes pixels, not OAM geometry, pose commands, animation scripts, collision or actor behavior. Up to 64 Scene working slots remain the shared limit; opening an additional art view refuses when no slot is available rather than deleting work.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The original WIP signature failure is corrected by initializing the dedicated view's zero collision-property array before signing it. Scene selection already installs that array; merely opening the view must not become a hidden edit. Unsupported fields still block native building and cannot be re-signed by opening another resource.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-14-run-a-concurrent-glass-joe-sequence-and-explicit-experiments\">14. Run a concurrent Glass Joe sequence and explicit experiments\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Choose \u003cstrong>Recovered content \u2192 Open Glass Joe native replay\u003c/strong>. The controls appear above the emulator canvas and remain available while it runs. Each UI Run loads your \u003cstrong>current native output\u003c/strong>, replaces the emulator session and uses the existing Timeline's bounded controller scripting. It pauses at the result. The project and preserved source remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Selection\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What the engine runs\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Declared intervention\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Reset \u2192 opening \u2192 punch \u2192 dodge \u2192 stance\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original startup/title/fight opening; A body punch, Right dodge and return to idle, including simultaneous opponent, HUD, CHR/scroll and Audio work\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Controller input only; no RAM patch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Seeded opponent knockdown \u2192 count \u2192 get-up\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native hit/knockdown, referee counts 1\u20134 and opponent get-up\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>After the real opening: target HP 1, clock near the first taunt; after knockdown, explicit native get-up target 4\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Seeded Mac knockdown \u2192 count-out\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native opponent hit, Mac knockdown, referee counts 1\u201310 and loss phase\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>After the real opening: player target HP 1 and clock near the first attack\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Seeded round end \u2192 round two\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original 3:00 round-end controller, break/continuation and active second round\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>After the real opening: set the clock to 2:59 with its declared fraction; Start pulses continue through the break\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>Use \u003cstrong>Load current build and replay\u003c/strong>, watch the milestones and evidence summary, and use \u003cstrong>Stop replay\u003c/strong> to interrupt. \u003cstrong>Restore fight start\u003c/strong> uses the Timeline-owned physical checkpoint immediately before the experiment. The evidence panel retains the observed experiment; the emulator's frame/readout shows the restored current state. A new ROM/session invalidates the old checkpoint. The API additionally supports \u003ccode>run(kind, {reuseReady: true})\u003c/code> for repeating a case from a matching, source-guarded ready checkpoint without replaying the opening; each report records whether it was reused.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The original controller routine requires stable input across consecutive polls; a one-frame press may never become a new button-history event. The replay holds Start/A for four frames and Right for twelve. It does not write fake pose states to pretend a normal sequence happened.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Count decoding uses RAM \u003ccode>$C1\u003c/code> for the count selector: \u003ccode>$9A\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>$A3\u003c/code> mean counts 1 through 10. \u003ccode>$C2\u003c/code> is the referee's X position in this context. An earlier research note named that coordinate as the count; the original initialization/drawing routines and observed count progression resolve the distinction. Do not treat a large coordinate such as \u003ccode>$E3\u003c/code> as count four.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Temporary seed values, before/after bytes, frame numbers, state/health/clock transitions, video hashes and APU-write activity are included in the evidence. The original CPU/NMI/mapper/PPU/APU run concurrently. This is bounded playback on Forge's approximate MMC2 model, not a cycle-accurate hardware result or newly supported runtime Scene capture. Native animation/AI/damage editing and native Audio writing remain separate work.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node reviews/punch-out-native-workflows-1/verify_art.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-native-workflows-1/verify_replay.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-native-workflows-1/verify_art_playback.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-native-workflows-1/verify_replay_guards.cjs /path/to/private-roms\npython3 reviews/punch-out-native-workflows-1/run_checks.py\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>The export/playback check opens edited CHR/palette output as a new native source, runs its actual original opening and fight sequence, checks the live PPU pattern byte and palette entry, compares pixels and retains Audio. It does not certify all art changes, all fights, external emulators, listening or physical hardware.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-15-use-the-adopted-retro-workstation-interface\">15. Use the adopted Retro Workstation interface\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Click \u003cstrong>Retro Workstation\u003c/strong> in the toolbar to enable the supplied skin; choose \u003cstrong>Compact\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>Comfortable\u003c/strong>. The default remains off and clicking again restores the original appearance. Appearance preferences are local settings, excluded from saved project data. All advanced tabs and existing controls remain accessible.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Resources\u003c/strong> opens the provided read-only relationship explorer; \u003cstrong>Connected Workspace\u003c/strong> offers context and shortcuts into the existing owners. Neither is a second asset store or a universal importer. The metasprite banner identifies the actual selected pose's source Scene; a different source is not an inferred sprite/collision assignment.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The kit's Zelda II native-overworld routes are also ported: the Area Library can reopen a linked native World or request the exact matching source when investigation has discarded its transient bytes. Revision/identity and stale-selection checks remain in place. These navigation fixes do not grant another game's missing terrain writer.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The supplied v0.5 browser evidence applies to that supplied HTML. The current modular integration has its own checks; any remaining browser/device acceptance is listed separately in the review notes. The source ports live under \u003ccode>app/src/ui/\u003c/code>, and the deterministic source manifest includes them. Directly replacing this app with the older review HTML would discard the new Punch-Out work, so continue from the integrated output.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-16-move-a-native-glass-joe-full-pose\">16. Move a native Glass Joe full pose\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>This workflow writes the two signed position operands of a full pose. It moves the graphic relative to the original moving opponent anchor. It does not move the opponent's collision/attack rules or change animation timing. Large shifts can separate the visible boxer from those original rules; inspect the built game.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open a supported Punch-Out ROM or its saved native Forge project. Choose \u003cstrong>Recovered content \u2192 Native pose positions \u00b7 Glass Joe\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select one of the 30 full poses, \u003ccode>$00\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>$3A\u003c/code> in steps of two. Choose \u003cstrong>Edit native Glass Joe pose position\u003c/strong>. The existing Metasprites editor opens a dedicated native frame and its source-art Scene. Opening an unchanged view produces the same ROM bytes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In \u003cstrong>NATIVE GLASS JOE POSE POSITION\u003c/strong>, change \u003cstrong>Native X / Y\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Apply native pose position\u003c/strong>. Values are signed offsets from -128 through 127. For pose \u003ccode>$00\u003c/code>, the original values are X = -16 and Y = -96. X = -14 moves that pose two pixels right relative to its native anchor. Only game states that use this particular full-pose record change; incremental/delta poses have separate source records.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply moves every original piece together in one Undo step. Undo/Redo retains the canonical frame; after undoing creation of a frame, reselect its native shortcut to restore the browsing selection. The frame's cyan origin and ordinary coordinates remain canonical Metasprite data. A uniform move through the composer, or a frame-origin change, can also express the same position. Individual piece drags, tile changes, flips, reordering and piece insertion/deletion remain savable but refuse native building.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Check native pose build\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong> or download the built ROM. Inspect the selected pose in its actual intro/fight/knockdown context. A full-pose position does not rewrite the delta-pose records used by many active animation updates.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Save Project As and reopen. Reopen the native pose shortcut to see its offsets. Build again and compare the exported ROM. Import that output as a new native source: its edited offsets become the new source baseline, and an unchanged rebuild should match exactly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Physical CHR/color edits and the arena writer compose with pose placement. The dedicated pose Scene snapshots current encoded art when first opened; its copied pixels/palettes are not a second art writer. Continue using the physical native art views to edit game graphics.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-independent-re-hex-and-source-development-for-pose-positions\">Independent RE, hex and source development for pose positions\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>The Glass Joe full-pose pointer table is in physical PRG bank 0. A pose record starts with signed X and Y bytes; subsequent fields point to shared geometry/attribute/tile streams. For selector \u003ccode>$00\u003c/code>, the position bytes are at file offsets \u003ccode>$009C\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>$009D\u003c/code>, including the 16-byte iNES header. An external hex edit from \u003ccode>$F0\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>$F2\u003c/code> at \u003ccode>$009C\u003c/code> expresses the same two-pixel X movement. Keep the original separate, change only the verified operand, compare the binary diff, and test the actual record's game use. These file offsets are for the two supported revisions, not a universal Punch-Out format guarantee.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose.js\u003c/code> reads the pointer graph, converts canonical placement to signed native operands, and executes the original shape reader to check the candidate. \u003ccode>services/punch-out-pose-project.js\u003c/code> creates the ordinary Scene/Metasprite views and registers exclusive two-byte ownership with NativeResourceProject. Its optional \u003ccode>metaspriteViews\u003c/code> hook substitutes only declared canonical positions for signature comparison; the encoder still rejects every unsupported piece edit. \u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose-ui.js\u003c/code> performs guarded ProjectStore transactions. Add these modules to the source manifest in dependency order and re-run the bundler when changing the HTML.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>No schema bump, copied ROM in the HTML, alternate asset store or second live CPU is involved. Explicit incremental positions are covered in section 17. Other opponents, position-less incremental records, shared geometry streams, animation/timing, AI/damage/collision, native Audio and complete game conversion remain separate work. The original shape reader and representative live original-engine playback are different checks; neither establishes hardware or browser listening acceptance.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node reviews/punch-out-pose-position-1/verify_pose.cjs /path/to/private-roms /path/to/previous-startup-fixed.html\nnode reviews/punch-out-pose-position-1/verify_pose_playback.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nNODE_PATH=/path/to/jsdom/node_modules node reviews/punch-out-pose-position-1/verify_pose_dom.cjs /path/to/private-roms\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-17-edit-native-idle-and-incremental-poses\">17. Edit native idle and incremental poses\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The position picker now includes \u003cstrong>30 full poses and five explicit incremental poses\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>$84\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$C2\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$C4\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>$C6\u003c/code>. The incremental reader retains the original preceding OAM instead of starting each frame from empty sprite memory. Its preview comes from a declared native state/update with the original full pose \u003ccode>$00\u003c/code> and fight configuration as initial conditions. The status shows that state, update and actual native opponent anchor. The anchor can move during the state; the source offsets and canonical pose remain relative to that observed anchor.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-move-the-idle-cycle-in-one-transaction\">Move the idle cycle in one transaction\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open a supported original or saved native project. Click \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong> near \u003cstrong>Area library\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Native pose positions \u00b7 Glass Joe\u003c/strong> is expanded near the top, above the source-reference pose preview. Choose \u003cstrong>Glass Joe \u00b7 incremental $C0 \u00b7 idle\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Edit native Glass Joe pose position\u003c/strong>. The separate \u003cstrong>Pose $0000\u003c/strong> picker below browses source references.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>An unchanged view rebuilds byte-for-byte. The original \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code> offsets are X=-14 and Y=-97. Its source Scene contains current encoded sprite art/colors; its Metasprite owns the editable position. Copied recovered reference frames still have their earlier meaning.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check \u003cstrong>Keep idle poses together ($C0/$C2/$C4; adds missing views)\u003c/strong>. Set \u003cstrong>Native X\u003c/strong> to -12, leave Y=-97, and \u003cstrong>Apply native pose position\u003c/strong>. The same +2 movement is applied to the current positions of all three idle records. Starting from an untouched source, their X values become -12, -14 and -13; their different original Y values retain the native bob. Earlier deliberate differences are preserved when moving an already-edited set.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Missing idle views are added as ordinary Scenes/Metasprites. Creation plus movement is one project transaction: one Undo returns to the pre-apply project, and Redo restores the entire change. An invalid group target or insufficient asset slots refuses the whole operation before the UI commits it. Opening \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code> initially is its own earlier Undo step.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use \u003cstrong>Check native pose build\u003c/strong>, Save Project As, reopen, reselect \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code>, and check the offsets. Save the original, project JSON and generated output separately.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build &amp; Run, or choose \u003cstrong>Open Glass Joe native replay \u2192 Reset \u2192 opening \u2192 punch \u2192 dodge \u2192 stance \u2192 Load current build and replay\u003c/strong>. This case uses real controller input from reset. Inspect the idle cycle during the fight; it can use these incremental records after the separate full-pose intro.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Export the native ROM and reimport it in this build. An unchanged rebuild should match the exported bytes. Reopen the native pose view to inspect its new source baseline. The Audio library still contains 84 entries; check source audition and edited-game Audio on your device.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>The checkbox appears only for \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$C2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>$C4\u003c/code>. Leave it unchecked to move just the selected record. \u003ccode>$84\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>$C6\u003c/code> have individual native position views. Every edited physical record affects \u003cstrong>all\u003c/strong> game paths that use it; one representative preview does not isolate a copy of that record to a single animation.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Earlier recovered state Animations and reference poses remain snapshots of the preserved source. Inspect native edits through the owned position views and the current-build replay. Editing a copied Animation does not patch the original state program, and the grouped position operation does not replace that program with an invented three-frame loop.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-follow-a-pose-into-chr-and-native-pixel-editing\">Follow a pose into CHR and native pixel editing\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>From the native position controls in \u003cstrong>Metasprites\u003c/strong>, choose \u003cstrong>View pose sprite tiles\u003c/strong>, or click \u003cstrong>CHR SHEET\u003c/strong> while its source Scene is selected. The sheet selects \u003cstrong>Sprite Bank ($1000)\u003c/strong> automatically. It shows the component tiles, not the assembled boxer. Moving native X/Y changes placement; the tile pixels themselves stay the same.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A context strip above the sheet identifies the pose and its physical bank. Its \u003cstrong>Show pose sprite tiles\u003c/strong> button restores the sprite buffer after a deliberate \u003cstrong>BG Bank ($0000)\u003c/strong> selection. Manual buffer selection still works within the current sheet; unrelated authoring Scenes retain their existing selection.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Edit native CHR bank 29\u003c/strong> in that strip, or \u003cstrong>Edit native sprite artwork\u003c/strong> in the pose controls. The exact bank comes from the verified pose record, not a guessed global bank choice. This opens the existing dedicated physical-bank Scene and selects \u003cstrong>BG Bank ($0000)\u003c/strong>, because that Scene's background CHR buffer owns the physical ROM pixels even when the game uses them for sprites.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select a tile and paint through the ordinary CHR pixel editor. Undo/Redo, Save Project As/reopen, then Build &amp; Run or export/reimport. The native write affects every use of that physical bank. Use \u003cstrong>Open native pose position\u003c/strong> to return to the originating pose during this navigation; the existing Metasprites controls also provide the two artwork shortcuts.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Earlier pose-source tiles remain their original snapshot; returning does not silently replace or re-sign reference art. Inspect changed pixels through the dedicated physical-bank Scene, a fresh fight reference or current-build replay. Unsupported pixel edits made in the pose's copied sprite buffer still save but refuse native builds; Undo those edits before opening a native writer.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>For developers, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose-ui.js\u003c/code> owns the sheet context and editor shortcuts, \u003ccode>PunchOutPoseProject.describe\u003c/code> supplies the source-verified \u003ccode>chrBank4KiB\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>re/punch-out-art-ui.js\u003c/code> selects the dedicated writer's background buffer. These are view/navigation changes. Physical pixel ownership, pose position ownership, project schemas, source bytes and the emulator remain with their existing owners.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-vertical-movement-and-native-priority\">Vertical movement and native priority\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>The original \u003ccode>$D043\u003c/code> routine compares native \u003cstrong>OAM Y with \u003ccode>$50\u003c/code> (80)\u003c/strong> for the \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code> family. Pieces at or below that raw OAM boundary receive the background-priority bit; their visible top row is OAM Y+1. Moving the pose vertically can therefore put parts of it behind the ring graphics. The canonical preview derives the same \u003ccode>behind\u003c/code> flags for its declared native context. The writer changes only X/Y data bytes; it leaves that routine and the attribute streams intact.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a controlled example, select \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code>, uncheck the idle-group option and change Y from -97 to -103. This moves the selected pose six pixels up and crosses the divider for the preview's first row. Undo restores the previous position and derived flags. In a different game state the moving anchor can put the same record at another screen height, so verify actual playback. Manually changing priority, flips, palettes, piece tiles/order or individual geometry is outside this writer; saving preserves such work and native builds refuse unsupported changes. Use the native X/Y controls for movements that need priority recomputation.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Native position changes retain the original attack/damage/collision code and timing. Moving art can separate it from the original hit rules. This is position editing, not a native hitbox, animation-program or complete-fight editor.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-inspect-or-hex-edit-the-exact-native-operands\">Inspect or hex-edit the exact native operands\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>All offsets below are \u003cstrong>physical file offsets including the 16-byte iNES header\u003c/strong>, for the supported US PRG0/PRG1 revisions. They are not CPU/RAM addresses; \u003ccode>$0200\u003c/code> in the last row is a file byte, not live OAM RAM.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Selector\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Explicit-position flag (protected)\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Writable X / Y file offsets\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Declared preview\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$84\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01DE\u003c/code> = \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01DF\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>$01E0\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>State \u003ccode>$03\u003c/code>, update 6\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01E9\u003c/code> = \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01EA\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>$01EB\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>State \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>, update 0\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C2\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01F0\u003c/code> = \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01F1\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>$01F2\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>State \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>, update 11\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C4\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01F7\u003c/code> = \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01F8\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>$01F9\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>State \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>, update 6\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C6\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01FE\u003c/code> = \u003ccode>$01\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01FF\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>$0200\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>State \u003ccode>$05\u003c/code>, update 10\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>For the idle X+2 example, an external hex editor makes exactly three changes on an untouched supported source:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>File offset\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Original\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Edited\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01EA\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$F2\u003c/code> (-14)\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$F4\u003c/code> (-12)\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code> X+2\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01F1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$F0\u003c/code> (-16)\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$F2\u003c/code> (-14)\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C2\u003c/code> X+2\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$01F8\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$F1\u003c/code> (-15)\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$F3\u003c/code> (-13)\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>$C4\u003c/code> X+2\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>Keep flags, pointer words, geometry/tile streams, state commands and other bytes unchanged. Compare the binary diff, reimport the edited file and test it in the original engine. The new derivative fingerprint masks exactly these ten additional operand bytes on top of the earlier bounded ranges. Recognition is not permission for a brush or encoder to write arbitrary bytes. Earlier full-pose-only builds do not recognize these new incremental derivatives; their supported unchanged-source projects still open here.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Records such as \u003ccode>$80\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$82\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$C8\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$CA\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>$CC\u003c/code> inherit position instead of carrying these two explicit operands. They are not added as fake independent X/Y resources. Recover their preceding state, inspect actual table bounds and follow the source relationship before implementing another writer. Shared geometry, timing/control flow, other opponents and native Audio writing remain separate work.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-modify-the-html-or-extend-this-method\">Modify the HTML or extend this method\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose.js\u003c/code> declares the five verified pointer/flag/operand records and preview contexts. It calls the existing \u003ccode>PunchOutStates\u003c/code> tracer, uses the observed anchor, validates whole-pose translation and derives only the original C-family priority bit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-pose-project.js\u003c/code> keeps the existing encoder registration and creates ordinary assets. Incremental Scenes use \u003ccode>captureMeta.punchOutIncrementalPose\u003c/code>; full views keep \u003ccode>punchOutPose\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>moveIdle\u003c/code> returns one detached project containing all three canonical changes. The UI performs the usual guarded ProjectStore/history transaction.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose-ui.js\u003c/code> adds the five choices, conditional idle checkbox and observed-context status to the existing Metasprites controls. Resources, Connected Workspace, the compact appearance and editor shortcuts retain their owners.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>re/native-punch-out-resources.js\u003c/code> admits the exact new derivative operands. NativeResourceProject still checks projection signatures and disjoint byte ownership, and the pose encoder independently refuses unsupported canonical fields. Art, palette and arena writers compose through their existing ranges; EmulatorBridge/Timeline runs the current native output.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>For a direct standalone experiment, save another HTML copy, locate the module's opening comment and edit that module's code. For reproducible source development, edit the corresponding \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code> files, run \u003ccode>python3 app/tools/bundle.py\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>python3 tools/sync_main_entry.py\u003c/code>; the root and generated HTML should match. New records require verified pointer/flag structure, correct preceding OAM/anchor context, a bounded inverse writer, protected-byte checks, old-project/reader checks, export/reimport and real original-engine verification. Expanding a hash mask alone does not supply those capabilities.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node reviews/punch-out-incremental-pose-1/verify_incremental.cjs /path/to/private-roms /path/to/previous-full-pose.html\nNODE_PATH=/path/to/jsdom/node_modules node reviews/punch-out-incremental-pose-1/verify_incremental_dom.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-incremental-pose-1/verify_idle_playback.cjs /path/to/private-roms\npython3 reviews/punch-out-incremental-pose-1/run_checks.py\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>These checks separate bounded native state/shape execution, actual project/UI lifecycle, parsed-DOM controls and live exported/reimported original-engine OAM/video. Parsed DOM uses canvas/timer stubs; Forge's MMC2 timing remains approximate. Browser layout/listening, controllers, independent-emulator and physical-hardware acceptance remain owner checks. No ROM, private saved commercial project, game frame or Audio payload is included in the kit.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-18-find-the-supported-native-editing-workflow\">18. Find the supported native editing workflow\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>After opening the recovered Punch-Out project, the working-context ribbon reads \u003cstrong>Punch-Out \u00b7 bounded native editing\u003c/strong>. Choose \u003cstrong>Native tools\u003c/strong>, or \u003cstrong>Connected Workspace \u2192 Native tools\u003c/strong>. The same project scope appears in Project investigation, Area Library and native preflight.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Native tool\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Where the shortcut leads\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What changes native output\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native source pages\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Investigated source in Area Library\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Available staging records through their native block controls\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Glass Joe pose positions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Expanded native pose picker, focused for explicit selection\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Supported whole-pose X/Y operands, including the grouped idle move in section 17\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Physical CHR and fight palettes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Expanded bank/palette picker\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The explicitly opened physical bank or palette table; source reference copies stay snapshots\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Shared arena layout\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The existing recovered arena Scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Tile placement and attribute palette IDs within its native compressed capacity\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>The pose/art shortcuts show the picker without adding a Scene or silently choosing a physical resource. Opening a chosen editing resource uses its existing explicit transaction and Undo behavior. An available writer describes the fields it supports; it does not certify every current edit. Use \u003cstrong>Code \u2192 Preflight Build\u003c/strong>, then save/reopen, build and verify the visible result in the original engine as described above.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The generated \u003cstrong>Build Scene Set\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>Include first 7\u003c/strong>, Scene Build/Order and ROM configuration controls are unavailable in native projects. Native output follows preserved source bindings and registered writers; marking a source view as included is not a native edit. In original NES Forge projects, generated packed builds still use the seven-Scene selection. World output follows saved World/player dependencies, and unpacked custom assembly uses active CHR buffers.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Native animation/AI/collision and native Audio writing remain separate work. The capability page changes navigation and guidance; it adds no new native writer. Independent RE, assembly, external hex edits and direct HTML/source modifications remain available through the general developer guide.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-19-follow-a-pose-its-tiles-and-the-shared-native-writers\">19. Follow a pose, its tiles, and the shared native writers\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>A physical CHR bank is a collection of indexed pixel patterns. It is not a character folder. Glass Joe and Don Flamenco use physical bank \u003cstrong>29\u003c/strong> in observed source contexts. Seeing Don Flamenco artwork in that whole bank is expected. It does not establish that every tile belongs to both boxers or that every pixel change affects both.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-what-each-resource-represents\">What each resource represents\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Resource\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Canonical owner / source\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What editing it does\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Selected pose\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>S.metasprites\u003c/code>, owned by the existing Metasprite editor and ProjectStore\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Stores piece tile IDs, palette IDs, flips, priority and positions. Only supported Glass Joe whole-pose positions have native writeback here.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Pose-source Scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Explicit \u003ccode>frame.sceneId\u003c/code>; copied \u003ccode>sprChr\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>sprPal\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Supplies the pose's reference pixels and colors. Direct unsupported pixel/color changes remain savable but block native building.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Pose tile footprint\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Read-only projection of the selected pose's pieces\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Shows tile IDs, piece references, palette and flips, including both halves of an 8\u00d716 piece. It adds no Scene OAM.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Whole bank\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>All 256 tile slots in the current Scene buffer\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Exposes the complete indexed sheet. The whole bank can include artwork outside the selected pose or character.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Physical CHR write owner\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing dedicated \u003ccode>punchOutArt\u003c/code> Scene, kind \u003ccode>chr\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Its \u003cstrong>BG\u003c/strong> CHR buffer writes the selected 4096-byte native bank. Shared uses of those bytes change together.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Fight palette write owner\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing dedicated \u003ccode>punchOutArt\u003c/code> Scene, kind \u003ccode>palette\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Writes one native fight palette table through its existing codec. Some original game phases override table colors.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Source identity\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing native receipt and investigation SHA-256\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Identifies the preserved ROM. A source relationship alone does not certify a native encoder or a build.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Known native consumers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bounded execution of original full-pose shape records\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reports observed references to a bank/tile. It does not add characters, poses or animations to the project.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>ROM bank chooser\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing independent browser over imported raw banks\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Merely selecting a bank does not change the displayed Scene buffer. The arrow buttons explicitly copy pixels into BG or Sprite buffers.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Audio library\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing title-specific source decoder and canonical authored Audio\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Available independently. A pose has no invented song binding. Native Audio writing remains separate.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-inspect-edit-and-verify-through-the-visual-workflow\">Inspect, edit and verify through the visual workflow\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open the supported ROM or saved native project. Use \u003cstrong>Native tools \u2192 Glass Joe pose positions\u003c/strong>, choose \u003cstrong>incremental $C0 \u00b7 idle\u003c/strong>, and open the position view. The native controls still handle whole-pose X/Y.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Click either \u003cstrong>Resources\u003c/strong> entry while in \u003cstrong>Metasprites\u003c/strong>. Resources selects the actual pose even if the active Scene is independently browsing another source. Its isolated preview uses the existing canonical pose renderer: piece order, palette IDs, horizontal/vertical flips and transparent pixels. Background priority needs a background to judge in game.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect the pose's dependencies. Its source Scene, sprite CHR, sprite palettes, preserved source identity, physical CHR bank and fight palette writer are distinct links. Source-receipt edges are labeled. Browsing these links adds no assets. \u003cstrong>Follow selection across editors\u003c/strong> remains an explicit option; selecting a pose with Follow on selects that exact pose and its source Scene.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Used pose tiles\u003c/strong> in Resources, or \u003cstrong>View pose sprite tiles\u003c/strong> in the pose controls. In CHR, set \u003cstrong>Tile view \u2192 Used by selected pose\u003c/strong>. \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code> contains \u003cstrong>52 pieces and 38 distinct tile IDs\u003c/strong>. Unreferenced slots are masked while their original grid indices stay fixed. \u003cstrong>Whole bank / Scene buffer\u003c/strong> restores the full sheet. These view controls do not change pixels or build bytes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Select a used tile. \u003cstrong>Pose use\u003c/strong> supplies its actual palette context instead of claiming it is unused because Scene OAM is empty. The sheet and pixel editor retain unflipped source indices. The small appearance canvases show recorded palette, H/V flips, priority and, for 8\u00d716 pieces, the displayed top/bottom half. A tile can have several appearances. Changing the preview is separate from changing palette data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Inspect known native uses of this tile\u003c/strong>. For bank 29, tile \u003ccode>$08\u003c/code> has observed Glass Joe full-pose consumers; tile \u003ccode>$53\u003c/code> (83) has Don Flamenco first-fight/rematch consumers; \u003ccode>$FF\u003c/code> has observed consumers from all three configurations. A reference can point to a blank or transparent pattern. The current source inventory decodes \u003cstrong>367 initial-mode full poses\u003c/strong>, with \u003cstrong>176 needing other context\u003c/strong>. Incremental states, alternate banking contexts, backgrounds and hidden uses are not exhaustively covered. No observed consumer means \u003cstrong>unknown beyond this coverage\u003c/strong>, not safe or unused.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Edit native CHR bank 29\u003c/strong>. This explicitly opens the registered physical writer; if needed, its dedicated Scene is added in one ordinary Undo transaction. The writer uses the BG buffer even for sprite art. The selected pose's tile footprint remains available when the physical source matches. Its appearance previews now read \u003cstrong>current physical pixels with the selected pose's snapshot colors\u003c/strong>. The pose-source Scene keeps its copied art.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Paint a pixel in the ordinary pixel editor. Inspect known consumers before changing additional tiles. Undo and Redo, then Build &amp; Run or export. Native output changes only within that physical bank's owned bytes. Use \u003cstrong>Edit native fight palette\u003c/strong> for exported fight colors; a physical-bank display palette is not a native color-table edit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Return to the native pose view and apply the grouped idle X=-12 example from section 17. Build again. Position operands and physical art writes compose through their existing separate byte spans. Save Project As, reopen in a fresh document, build and compare the generated bytes. An earlier source snapshot is not silently refreshed or accepted as a new baseline.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verify the exported game in the original engine and another emulator: enter Glass Joe's fight, inspect idle movement and edited art, then inspect other observed consumers of the changed tile. Check the relevant fight palettes and Audio. Browser/DOM checks and a binary diff do not replace a full fight, listening, controller or hardware acceptance pass.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-work-independently-with-code-hex-or-the-standalone-html\">Work independently with code, hex or the standalone HTML\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>Native CHR file offsets include the 16-byte iNES header. For the supported layout:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>bank start = $20010 + physical_bank * $1000\ntile start = bank start + tile_id * 16\nlow bitplane row = tile start + row       (row 0..7)\nhigh bitplane row = tile start + row + 8\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>Bank 29 therefore starts at \u003cstrong>\u003ccode>$3D010\u003c/code>\u003c/strong>. These are file offsets, distinct from editor BG \u003ccode>$0000\u003c/code> / Sprite \u003ccode>$1000\u003c/code> labels and live PPU mappings. A pixel's two bits are read from the same bit position in the two row bytes. A hex edit can change one or both bytes. Preserve a source backup, compare the diff, reimport through verified recognition, and test all relevant observed uses. The source marker or a broader identity mask never authorizes writes outside an encoder's owned spans.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To extend or restyle the connections, start with these commented modules:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Module under \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Responsibility / connection\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/resource-context.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Read-only editor selection, existing source receipts, physical writer routes, Metasprite footprint and bounded native consumer observations. Disposable decoded evidence cache only.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/scene-context.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Displays source identity for newer native views and opens the matching existing source investigation. It does not invent a stage or Audio binding.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>ui/resource-relationships.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Projects existing IDs/receipts into the explorer, previews poses through the canonical renderer, and explicitly opens existing editors/writers. Physical nodes are derived views.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>ui/connected-workspace.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Uses the shared current-editor selection before opening Resources. Metasprites no longer falls back to the unrelated active Scene.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>ui/resource-usage.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Tile-view controls, appearance canvases, known-consumer report, palette route and independent ROM-browser explanation. UI state is not serialized.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/04-saveas-chr.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing CHR color resolver, pixel editor and sheet hit tests. Uses pose pieces when applicable; keeps raw pixel orientation and stable tile indices.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-art-project.js\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>re/punch-out-art.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing validated physical bank/palette owners and codecs. The workflow changes do not expand their writable fields.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-pose-project.js\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing whole-pose writer, incremental context and idle transaction. Geometry/animation/hit rules retain their limits.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>For a direct HTML experiment, make a separate copy, search for a module's opening comment or a control ID such as \u003ccode>chrUsageScope\u003c/code>, and edit that module's HTML-building code or styles. For maintained source, edit \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, rebuild the ordinary and optional hosted manifests, and check both generated outputs. Both builds share these modules; do not reintroduce a second editor snapshot. Ordinary projects remain schema 12 and the reader ceiling stays 16.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The generic footprint/selection projection also works for authored poses. Additional native adapters need verified source-to-physical mappings; a normalized captured tile index cannot be assumed to equal a native ROM tile ID. Unknown links must stay unknown. Keep canonical assets, history, source validation and byte writers with their existing owners when adding another title.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-20-resume-the-same-pose-and-native-artwork-workspace\">20. Resume the same pose and native artwork workspace\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>, browser project saves, recovery saves and Export's \u003ccode>project.json\u003c/code> now include optional browsing preferences. Opening the JSON in a fresh application restores the selected pose and piece, core editor tab, selected World, CHR buffer/tile and whole-bank/used-pose view. Pose selection remains independent from the active Scene when that was intentional. The selected World is a browsing choice; World Runtime still uses its separately configured start World and spawn.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Native tools \u2192 Glass Joe pose positions \u2192 incremental $C0 \u00b7 idle\u003c/strong>. Apply the position example if desired. Select the pose piece you want to resume, then \u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>. Choose a location or explicitly use the browser-download fallback.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open the current HTML in a fresh document. Use \u003cstrong>Open Project\u003c/strong> and choose that JSON. The Metasprites editor, \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code>, selected piece and current native offsets return. Resources follows that restored pose; its source Scene reference and physical write owners retain their existing meanings.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open the native CHR bank, choose \u003cstrong>Used by selected pose\u003c/strong>, select a tile, and save again. A fresh open returns to the physical bank's BG buffer and the same tile/pose footprint. \u003cstrong>Open native pose position\u003c/strong> derives its route from the restored canonical selection, so it works without earlier shortcut clicks in that document.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deliberately switching BG/Sprite buffers is also retained in new saves. The usual native entry default applies to older saves that lack the new preferences. The whole bank, used-pose mask and preview appearance change browsing; native writes still require the appropriate existing writer.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build before saving and after reopening. The native output should match. Undo/Redo and the original snapshot guards still apply. Copied source artwork is never refreshed or re-signed just to restore a selection.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>Older projects with no pose preference choose a pose only when exactly one uses the active Scene. If several match, the frame selector says \u003cstrong>Choose a pose\u2026\u003c/strong> and the editor explains the choice. If none matches, it stays explicitly unselected rather than displaying an unrelated first boxer. A valid saved pose can still have a different source Scene by design; that independent context is restored. Missing/stale/malformed view hints use the same bounded fallback, preserving assets and unresolved references.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>editorView\u003c/code> block is optional UI metadata. It is excluded from default authoring/build captures and native signatures; schema 12 and the reader ceiling of 16 remain. Older readers can discard these extra preferences while retaining the project. Browser Save/Load and explicit recovery restoration use the same owner as portable saves. A saved editor tab does not preserve emulator RAM or resume playback.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For developers, \u003ccode>services/editor-view.js\u003c/code> validates bounded preferences and coordinates existing UI owners. \u003ccode>services/project-store.js\u003c/code> owns capture/load/history; \u003ccode>Forge163.metasprites.restoreSelection\u003c/code> restores UI IDs without changing frames. \u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose-ui.js\u003c/code> resolves the return shortcut from the selected native pose and its verified physical bank. Change these commented modules, or locate their comments in a separate HTML copy, then rebuild both manifests. The general developer guide describes the optional JSON fields and compatibility boundaries.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-21-compare-previews-and-diagnose-a-refused-native-build\">21. Compare previews and diagnose a refused native build\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Resources now reads Scene pixels through the same compositor used by captured Scene editing, including background attributes, universal color, active OAM, 8\u00d716 halves, flips, transparency, priority, scanline limits and captured palette/mask states. Editor guides, entity boxes and selection outlines are omitted. A pose remains its own composed asset: it does not appear in a Scene unless that Scene actually has OAM placements.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The CHR resource view uses the CHR editor's existing appearance resolver. For the displayed Scene/bank it retains the current \u003cstrong>Scene colors (auto)\u003c/strong>, \u003cstrong>Selected palette\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>Pixel values\u003c/strong> mode and appearance choice. Browsing another Scene/bank uses that source's automatic usage colors without selecting it. Sprite index 0 is checkerboard. Tiles stay unflipped, and capture atlases follow the selected tile's page above 255. Preview modes do not recolor an asset.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-compare-a-snapshot-with-the-current-encoded-pose\">Compare a snapshot with the current encoded pose\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Glass Joe pose positions \u2192 incremental $C0 \u00b7 idle\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Resources\u003c/strong>. The first canvas shows the canonical pose with its copied source artwork.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Click \u003cstrong>Preview current native output\u003c/strong>. Forge runs the same compiler entry as Build &amp; Run (without reloading/normalizing the project) without downloading a ROM or starting playback, then decodes the supported pose from those exact output bytes. The second canvas uses encoded physical CHR and fight palettes. Its caption includes physical bank, output SHA-256 and scope.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open the physical CHR bank through its declared writer, paint a pixel, and optionally edit the fight palette through its own writer. Return to the pose and preview again. The output can differ while the first snapshot stays unchanged. Position edits compose through the same native build owner.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For an incremental pose, this preview uses the codec's declared state path. It is not a live fight frame and does not prove every phase, background-priority interaction or game-code palette override. Enter the fight in Build &amp; Run and inspect other known consumers as described in section 19.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>If the current project cannot compile, the preview displays the real refusal. It never substitutes the original ROM to hide unsupported edits. Open \u003cstrong>Project Health\u003c/strong> to inspect the build problem.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-find-the-field-blocking-output\">Find the field blocking output\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open \u003cstrong>Connected Workspace \u2192 Project Health\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Declared references\u003c/strong> describes asset links; \u003cstrong>Current ROM build\u003c/strong> starts as \u003cstrong>Not checked\u003c/strong>. Zero relationship issues does not certify a build.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Click \u003cstrong>Check current ROM build\u003c/strong>. This runs the existing full compiler without committing an artifact, downloading or changing emulator state. \u003cstrong>Compiler passed\u003c/strong> applies to that capture only. Edit the project and reopen/refresh Health to invalidate the previous result.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>On refusal, read the original backend message and any confirmed resource/field context. Examples: a recovered source page's \u003ccode>nt[0]\u003c/code>, a pose-source snapshot's \u003ccode>sprChr[8][0][0]\u003c/code>, a physical bank's incorrectly edited \u003ccode>sprChr\u003c/code>, or a native pose's unsupported piece geometry. \u003cstrong>Inspect this resource\u003c/strong> selects the named asset; \u003cstrong>Review supported native tools\u003c/strong> shows its available writers.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong> first if you want to keep unsupported work. Use ordinary Undo to remove the offending edit. Then make the intended native change with the proper writer: physical bank \u003cstrong>BG\u003c/strong> pixels, fight palette slots, native source blocks or complete-pose X/Y. Diagnostic navigation does not repair or discard anything automatically.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A native projection hash does not contain the original value of every field. A source-copy field is named only when substituting a reproducible known source value in a detached diagnostic candidate restores the saved hash. If that proof is unavailable, the report explicitly says it cannot locate the protected change. Preserve the project and trace edits through history or independent JSON/hex/code comparison; do not reset the saved signature to force acceptance.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Code \u2192 Preflight Build\u003c/strong> and failed \u003cstrong>Build / Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong> use the same structured refusal details. Preflight remains a planning check; the explicit Health button runs compilation. Save/reopen, compare bytes and test the exported game after resolving the issue.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"punch-out-change-the-html-or-extend-the-source\">Change the HTML or extend the source\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>Search the standalone HTML for \u003ccode>src/services/resource-preview.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Preview current native output\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fcw-check-build\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>src/services/build-diagnostics.js\u003c/code>. A separate HTML copy can change captions/layout for an experiment. Maintained changes belong in \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, followed by the existing native/root and hosted rebuild commands. Both builds share the same modules.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>File under \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What it does and connects to\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/resource-preview.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Supplies explicit read-only Scene/CHR context; compiles and decodes supported current-output poses through existing owners. No cached canonical output or snapshot refresh.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/04-saveas-chr.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Owns \u003ccode>indexFor\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>previewFor\u003c/code>, palette resolution and CHR drawing used by both editors and Resources.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/03-runtime-capture.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing \u003ccode>scenePixels\u003c/code> compositor; Resources consumes it without changing capture or emulator behavior.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/13-metasprites.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing pose compositor accepts an explicit detached source for output preview. Piece order, flips and transparency retain one renderer.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/build-diagnostics.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Formats actual errors and resource navigation. It grants no native write permission.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/build-services.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing BuildGraph/RuntimeCompiler; \u003ccode>checkCurrent\u003c/code> compiles without commit and binds the result to the captured input.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/native-resource-project.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing acceptance/hash/span owner. Adds field evidence only after a real refusal, without changing signature calculation.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-pose-project.js\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>punch-out-art-project.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing writer-owned source-view constructors supply reproducible diagnostic candidates and contextual errors. Accepted fields/spans do not expand.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>ui/resource-relationships.js\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>ui/connected-workspace.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Preview buttons, source/output captions, Health result and repair navigation. These UI results are disposable.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>Additional titles should supply context at their actual decoder/encoder refusal, not infer eligibility from Resources links. Unknown historical snapshots must remain unknown. This workflow adds no animation, AI, collision, combat, referee/round or Audio write capability.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"punch-out-22-identify-the-source-and-find-the-lesson-inside-forge\">22. Identify the source and find the lesson inside Forge\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Project lessons\u003c/strong> beside \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong> opens this maintained tutorial inside \u003cstrong>TECH DOCS\u003c/strong>, even from the standalone HTML with no network. \u003cstrong>Lesson for this resource\u003c/strong> in Resources follows its exact Scene/pose/tile context; the idle pose opens section 17 and CHR usage opens section 19. \u003cstrong>Lesson for this Audio source\u003c/strong> opens section 7 for the catalog being inspected, including an independent ROM. Choose another chapter or section with the reader's controls, use cross-chapter links, or \u003cstrong>Return to editor\u003c/strong>. Reading lessons does not add assets, change the native recipe or modify the source snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Source labels have specific meanings:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Label\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Available next step\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Current project source\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>This input's SHA-256 matches the source preserved by the active project and its owning session guard is current\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect its supported resources; the existing native writer still decides whether an edit builds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Independent source\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>You explicitly opened a source outside the active project's source/session\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect bytes or native cues and export evidence; conversion/import is still deliberate and guarded\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Detached source\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The previous project or source guard is no longer current\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Read the retained identity/evidence; stale intake and Audio operations are disabled\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Inspected project file\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The input is a saved project JSON, not a ROM catalog\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open it explicitly to replace the active workspace\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Independent multi-file workspace\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Separate source files, declarations, reports and source-ROM output\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Edit/build/export that workspace independently; it is not the project's assembly buffer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>An intake capability receipt is tied to the project snapshot at inspection. Editing or replacing that project invalidates its actions visibly and collapses the panel with a detached-input label. Expand it to review the old report, or open the input again. A native Audio catalog's source session has its own guard. After replacing Punch-Out with another project, an old catalog can remain for reference but cannot claim ready import/audition actions. Any pending staged import is discarded rather than retargeted.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To keep using that old ROM intentionally, choose \u003cstrong>Continue independent Audio inspection\u003c/strong>. Forge reopens its retained bytes through the existing Audio source loader. It does not load the emulator, attach the ROM to the new project or enable native Audio writing. The independent label stays visible; \u003cstrong>Import into tracker\u003c/strong> still requires a fresh conversion preview, destination/project guard and the existing explicit confirmation. \u003cstrong>Use current project source\u003c/strong> opens the active project's preserved ROM in the same Audio loader with its own epoch/source guard, without starting the emulator or changing assets. Project investigation also restores its catalog.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The reader's \u003cstrong>Active project output\u003c/strong> strip always describes the active build backend. An independent Punch-Out Audio lesson can therefore appear above an authored-project output strip; that does not grant Punch-Out a generated or foreign game's writer. The chapter selection and source role keep those contexts distinct.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For code/HTML changes, \u003ccode>services/source-context.js\u003c/code> is a read-only identity/role projection. \u003ccode>ui/source-context.js\u003c/code> refreshes the existing panels on project lifecycle and UI events. \u003ccode>re/project-gateway-ui.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/investigation-ui.js\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>audio/native-audio-library-ui.js\u003c/code> retain their original guarded session/transaction owners. \u003ccode>ui/project-lessons.js\u003c/code> owns only reading/navigation. Its content is generated from the same maintained Markdown as the offline handbook by \u003ccode>app/tools/render_project_tutorials.py\u003c/code>; edit the Markdown rather than hand-editing \u003ccode>src/docs/project-lessons-data.js\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/PUNCH_OUT.md", "sourceSha256": "902efa284ad32b8cefeebaf261b9278ecda24df6a8c2d180c377551ec57cf29d"}, {"id": "general", "title": "NES Forge developer and asset guide", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"general-nes-forge-developer-and-asset-workflow-guide\">NES Forge developer and asset workflow guide\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>This guide describes the current source checkpoint, using Punch-Out as a worked recovery example. It is intended for people who want to use the visual editors, investigate an unknown ROM, write an adapter, write 6502 code, patch bytes independently, or modify NES Forge itself. Game recognition is optional for general inspection. Each game's editable native output depends on the encoders actually installed for that revision.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The standalone HTML contains the application, not your subsequently saved project or an embedded commercial ROM. Keep the HTML, your project JSON, original input and any rebuilt output separately. An updated HTML can open your saved project; replacing the HTML does not transfer unsaved browser state automatically.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-1-choose-an-entry-point\">1. Choose an entry point\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Goal\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Starting workflow\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Result\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What must be verified\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Create original art or a game\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Start a separate authored project; use the asset tabs and chosen Forge runtime\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Canonical assets and a generated Forge ROM within that runtime's capabilities\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Build backend, references, limits and play behavior\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect an unfamiliar ROM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Open ROM / project \u2192 Inspect bytes / CHR; RE / DISASM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Physical layout, byte ranges, candidate disassembly, notes and explicit gaps\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Bank mapping, code/data distinction, runtime observations\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Edit a recognized game's supported data\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Prepare recovered resources \u2192 Import as new project; Area library or Recovered content\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source-preserving project, native recipes and/or editable copies\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The selected field has a native encoder; save/reopen; original-engine output\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Write 6502 code\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>CODE in a separate authored project; assembler templates\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Assembly source, symbols and emitted bytes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Reset/NMI/IRQ vectors, memory placement and live custom build settings\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Hex edit a ROM yourself\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Export/copy bytes to your external hex editor, or use a narrow supported native-command editor\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A separately patched ROM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>File offsets, bank mapping, aliases, checksums, execution and recognition after patching\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change the app\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Edit modular source and bundle, or edit a separate standalone HTML copy\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A modified NES Forge executable\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Syntax, UI lifecycle, saved projects, native output and retained features\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Reuse recovered material in a different engine\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Explicitly convert/import supported assets into an authored project\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Forge adaptations\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Conversion fidelity and the new runtime's rules; original-game patching is separate\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>The general-analysis hex pane currently displays bytes; it is \u003cstrong>not a general inline ROM hex editor\u003c/strong>. Reversible RAM experiments affect the loaded emulator session, not the source ROM. The dedicated native command-byte Audio editor is narrow and profile-specific; it does not provide a general Punch-Out Audio writer. These are different tools with different output ownership.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-2-understand-the-three-representations\">2. Understand the three representations\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Source:\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>importedRom\u003c/code> preserves the input bytes. In project JSON it is Base64; the live editor holds bytes. \u003ccode>nativeGame.sourceSha256\u003c/code> identifies those bytes. Never edit one without updating and validating the complete source descriptor and associated evidence.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Evidence:\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>investigation\u003c/code> and decoder catalogs describe observations, source ranges, profiles and gaps. They are not a second asset database or executable game source. Viewing a catalog does not mean every item has been materialized into an editing tab.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Canonical authoring assets:\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>scenes\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>worlds\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>metasprites\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>animations\u003c/code>, Audio collections and gameplay records are the editable project. Some are linked to native recipes; others are recovered copies or adaptations with no inverse writer.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>A useful recovery must state separately: \u003cem>recognized, decoded, materialized, editable, natively encodable, and verified in play\u003c/em>. A source can be recognized without complete assets. A recovered animation can be edited and saved without being patchable into the original game. A byte-preserving native build can succeed without showing that a new visual edit works.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ProjectStore\u003c/code> owns serialization and lifecycle. \u003ccode>AssetRegistry\u003c/code> resolves authoring references and reports missing/duplicate IDs. \u003ccode>BuildGraph\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>RuntimeCompiler\u003c/code> choose and execute the build path. Recovery adapters use those owners rather than creating another lifecycle or replacing the editor.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-3-complete-asset-map\">3. Complete asset map\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The fields below identify the important records, not every optional extension. Use a saved project produced by the current app as the schema example. \u003ccode>ProjectStore.prepareLoad()\u003c/code> and the relevant model validators are authoritative for exact accepted fields and limits.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Asset\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Canonical location and meaning\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Editor workflow\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Code to inspect first\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Native-ROM implication\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scenes[]\u003c/code>: stable ID, name, background, sprites, palette, entities, links, settings\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Select a Scene, edit its resources, save; use Area library for source pages\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/05-foundation.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/scene-context.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/area-library.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Only the adapter's supported fields become native edits\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Background CHR\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.chr[tile][y][x]\u003c/code>: 8\u00d78 pixel indices 0\u20133\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>CHR editor; select background bank and tile; change pixels\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/04-saveas-chr.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/re-native-assets.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Copied pixels are not a physical ROM patch without a CHR encoder\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Sprite CHR\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.sprChr\u003c/code>: separate sprite pixel bank\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Select sprite graphics in CHR; check the sprite palette and frame using it\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Same CHR owners; \u003ccode>re/punch-out-content.js\u003c/code> for native banks\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Physical bank/latch context and shared uses must be resolved before writing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Background arrangement\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.nt\u003c/code>: 960 tile references, 32\u00d730\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>SCENE paint, fill and eyedrop tools; choose the correct selected Scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/00-editor.js\u003c/code>, native resource/arena modules\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native tile IDs may differ from projected CHR atlas indices\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Attribute bytes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.attr\u003c/code>: 64 bytes selecting background palette IDs by 16\u00d716 quadrants\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Paint palette IDs with tiles; inspect neighboring cells\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Scene paint code; arena projection/encoder\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>This selects an existing palette; it does not change palette colors\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Palette colors\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.pal\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>scene.sprPal\u003c/code>: four color groups\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Palette picker, then inspect tiles and scenes sharing those colors\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Scene/CHR renderers and native decoder palette provenance\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Color-table writing is separate from attribute writing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Sprite placements\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.oam[]\u003c/code>: x/y, tile/tile2, attribute, height, active, optional provenance\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Sprite/Scene tools; verify flips, priority and size\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/00-editor.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-content.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original OAM is runtime state; editing a copied placement is not editing original animation code\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Metatile definitions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.metatiles[]\u003c/code>: groups of CHR tiles, palette/collision data\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Build/edit a metatile library, then place it on a map\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/07-metatiles.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A native game's block format may require an adapter-specific mapping\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Metatile map\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.metaMap\u003c/code>: 16\u00d715 metatile references for a Scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Edit using the metatile tools; verify underlying tile projection\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Same metatile owner\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Do not assume a visual block equals a native record\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Collision properties\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.tileProps\u003c/code> and applicable per-cell/metatile extensions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>COLLISION; assign properties and inspect runtime behavior\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Metatile/World compilers; \u003ccode>services/build-services.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>No collision meaning can be inferred reliably from tile appearance alone\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Metasprite pose\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>metasprites[]\u003c/code>: ID, \u003ccode>sceneId\u003c/code>, size, origin, pieces with offsets/palette/flips\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>METASPRITES; assemble pieces from sprite CHR\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/13-metasprites.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/punch-out-content-recovery.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A pose copy can be editable without a native pose writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Animation\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>animations[]\u003c/code>: timed frames, pose references, loop/state, boxes/events\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>ANIMATION; edit frames/durations and preview transitions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/14-composed-animation.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>legacy/16-model.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native timing/control-flow translation needs a separate writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Hitboxes/hurtboxes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Animation frame \u003ccode>hitboxes\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>hurtboxes\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Edit contact boxes at the relevant frame and test runtime support\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/16-model.js\u003c/code>, gameplay compiler/engine\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Editor boxes are not automatically the native game's collision routine\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Frame events\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Frame \u003ccode>events[]\u003c/code> with typed marker information\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Add/reorder events; inspect when the consuming runtime triggers them\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Same model plus Audio/gameplay runtime\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Observed native sound-request metadata is not an authored event by itself\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Scene entities\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.entities[]\u003c/code>: placed authoring objects and animation bindings\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Entity tools; place objects, bind states and destinations\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/18-entity-studio.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>legacy/16-model.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native object placement/AI formats are separate recovery work\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Gameplay actors\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>gameplayCore.actorDefinitions\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>actorInstances\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>behaviors\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Gameplay editor; choose supported components and typed behavior operations\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>gameplay/gameplay-model.js\u003c/code>, compiler/engine/UI\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Available authoring operations exceed the bounded compiled runtime subset; validate the backend\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Persistent variables\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>gameplayCore.persistentVariables\u003c/code> plus instance bindings\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Define type/scope, bind actors and test room/world transitions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Gameplay model/compiler/engine\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native save flags and RAM addresses require an explicit mapping\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Scene links / doors\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>scene.links\u003c/code>, entity destinations and corresponding World markers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Connect scenes; verify destination IDs and build inclusion\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Scene, entity and World services\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A destination coordinate alone does not establish reachable/safe native travel\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>World\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>worlds[]\u003c/code>: dimensions, \u003ccode>tilesetSceneId\u003c/code>, metatile map, rooms/markers/cameras\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>WORLD; choose tileset, paint, configure rooms and test transitions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>world/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>legacy/08-world.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>legacy/09-world-codec.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A source-native World adapter and a Forge-authored World are different build paths\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Runtime settings\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>gameplay\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>gameplayCore\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>worldRuntime\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>audioRuntime\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Select the intended mode and enable only supported runtime features\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Config/model modules; \u003ccode>services/build-services.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>These do not rewrite a preserved game's engine without an encoder\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Instruments\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>instruments[]\u003c/code>: duty/volume/arpeggio/pitch envelope data\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AUDIO instrument editor; audition in context\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>audio/audio-engine.js\u003c/code>, UI/compiler/import modules\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>A native envelope table may not have an equivalent canonical instrument\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Patterns\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>patterns[].rows[row][channel]\u003c/code>: notes, instruments, volume, effects fields\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AUDIO tracker note entry; inspect rows/channels and preview\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>audio/audio-ui.js\u003c/code>, engine/compiler\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Supported effects depend on the preview/compiler; storing fields does not imply execution\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Order list\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>orderList\u003c/code>: pattern selections over song sequence positions\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AUDIO sequence/order editing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Audio engine/compiler\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native loops, section dispatch and channel arbitration may not map 1:1\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Tempo/speed\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>tempo\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>speed\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>audRows\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AUDIO timing controls; compare expected musical timing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Audio model/compiler\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native update timing and per-driver duration tokens may differ\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>SFX\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>sfx[]\u003c/code> plus runtime scheduling/event references\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AUDIO SFX tools; inspect priority and channel use\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Audio compiler/engine/config\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Procedural native effects cannot always be represented as tracker notes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native Audio cues\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source-owned catalog, stream descriptors and sample references\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>AUDIO \u2192 ROM Audio Library \u2192 inspect/audition/explicit conversion\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-audio-*\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/punch-out-audio.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native audition, canonical adaptation and native rewriting are distinct\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>DPCM / direct PCM\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original sample bytes and/or DAC-producing native routine\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native audition where installed\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-audio-machine.js\u003c/code>, Punch-Out Audio module\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>No generic tracker PCM/sample authoring or Punch-Out native Audio writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Assembly\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>asmSource\u003c/code>; live assembler emits symbols/binary/write addresses\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>CODE; assemble, inspect errors/symbols and build in supported custom mode\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/00-editor.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/build-services.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Disassembly of native bytes is not a rebuildable assembly project\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Code/byte evidence\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Analysis sessions, annotations, ranges, candidate symbols\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>RE / DISASM; choose offsets, CPU origin and bank labels\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/re-analysis.js\u003c/code>, UI, source workspace modules\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>General analysis is read-only; exports/evidence do not patch ROMs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Raster state\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Optional Scene \u003ccode>raster\u003c/code> and capture/source metadata\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect whether recovery preserved bank/scroll/palette changes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/03-runtime-capture.js\u003c/code>, native renderers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Mid-frame behavior must remain modeled or explicitly unsupported\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native recipes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>nativeGame.edits\u003c/code> plus bindings and registered Scene writers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native brush or declared supported Scene edits\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/native-resource-project.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Recipes refer to physical operands; aliases must agree\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Source metadata\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>captureMeta\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>investigation\u003c/code>, source hashes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Inspect provenance and limits; preserve on save\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>ProjectStore and adapter validation\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Changing evidence/hashes to silence an error is not implementing an encoder\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>For a Scene currently selected in the UI, \u003ccode>S.chr\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>S.nt\u003c/code>, palettes and other top-level working arrays mirror its content. \u003ccode>saveCurrentScene()\u003c/code> synchronizes them before capture. Editing a detached project JSON is different from changing the live \u003ccode>S\u003c/code> buffers; avoid creating competing versions of the same Scene.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Raw CHR banks contain patterns. A sprite sheet is a view of those patterns; OAM places them; a metasprite assembles a pose; an animation sequences poses; an actor combines appearance and behavior; a complete scene adds background, UI, palette/bank/raster context and runtime relationships. Recovering one layer never proves the others exist.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-4-visual-asset-editing-end-to-end\">4. Visual asset editing, end to end\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-backgrounds-tiles-and-palettes\">Backgrounds, tiles and palettes\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Save a baseline project. Select the actual target Scene or source page. A fighter-source Scene intentionally has a blank background; an intro Scene is not the fight arena.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect its CHR and palette. If source graphics are banked, record physical bank and runtime latch/scroll context. Do not replace missing placement data with a tile sheet and call it a recovered scene.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Paint the Scene layout, or edit CHR pixels deliberately. Palette-ID painting changes attributes; the color picker changes palette colors. These require different native writers.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For a native project, check the declared supported fields. Punch-Out arena \u003ccode>nt\u003c/code>/visible attribute edits are encodable. For native artwork/color edits, open the dedicated physical CHR or fight-palette views described in section 15; direct edits to copied arena/fight-reference art remain unsupported. Unsupported edits remain savable but block native builds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo, Redo, save, reopen and rebuild. Enter the game state that uses the data. Test neighboring rows, page seams, offscreen dependencies and shared uses.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Re-import the rebuilt ROM if continuing from it. This checkpoint restores Punch-Out recognition for bounded arena derivatives; arbitrary patches outside recognized ranges still need adapter investigation.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>Background CHR pixel 0 is the background color selection; sprite pixel 0 is transparent. Sprite/background palettes and bank selection are separate. Some projected source Scenes can use more than 256 logical tiles to preserve bank-dependent rows; those logical indices cannot be written directly to an 8-bit native tile stream.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-sprites-metasprites-and-animation\">Sprites, metasprites and animation\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>Open \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong> for source poses or the regular editors for authored work. Select fight/pose, preview, then explicitly open a supported pose in Metasprites. Verify the pose's source Scene, sprite size, origin, piece order, flips, palette and CHR bank.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For timed source recovery: \u003cstrong>State animation excerpts \u2192 Trace state \u2192 Open recovered animation\u003c/strong>. Inspect assumptions and stop conditions. Frames changing size must retain the native tile stride; incremental poses must retain the preceding OAM. Adjust canonical frame timing or pieces, preview, save and reopen. This does not yet encode those changes into Punch-Out's original animation streams.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>When creating authored animation, keep stable metasprite IDs, reference their Scenes, set duration/loop behavior and bind entity states to animation IDs. Test hitboxes, hurtboxes and events in the intended runtime; the editor preview alone does not establish compiled behavior. Do not delete a referenced Scene or pose to make room without reviewing the references reported by AssetRegistry.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-worlds-collision-actors-and-interactions\">Worlds, collision, actors and interactions\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>For an authored World, establish the tileset Scene and metatile library before painting its map. Define rooms, markers, destinations and camera regions; test travel in both directions and after room reload. Enable the intended World/runtime settings. A saved marker is not evidence that a native game's original travel code uses it.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For authored collision, choose properties based on intended mechanics, then test actor movement/contact. Verify per-cell overrides and metatile projection where applicable. For actors, distinguish a reusable definition from placed instances. Keep variables typed and scoped, use only operations supported by the compiled backend, and test deterministic transition priority, spawn/despawn, slot reuse and persistence across rooms. Refer to \u003ccode>GameplayModel.runtimeProfile\u003c/code> for the bounded native runtime subset rather than assuming every UI operation is compiled.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For native projects, recover these domains from original tables/code independently. Tile appearance, pose geometry and enemy labels cannot establish physics, damage, AI or save-state semantics.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-audio\">Audio\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>On every project verify driver detection, cue inventory, native audition, explicit editable conversion where supported, save/reopen and preservation after Scene rebuilding. Empty/stop/unavailable cue slots should be labeled rather than counted as sounding music.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Native audition runs original driver code with native sample sources. Tracker conversion makes finite canonical patterns/instruments where representable; listen for differences in envelopes, pitch slides, channel ownership, noise, loops and DPCM. Save the project, reopen, audition again and compare Scene-edited output. Audio writing requires its own native encoder.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Punch-Out's startup PCM runs a separate one-shot routine across continuous CPU-cycle windows, with native bell writes. Select at least 199 updates for full isolated playback. Shorter windows report truncation. It is auditionable, but PCM tracker editing and native sample rewriting remain open.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-5-manual-re-workflow\">5. Manual RE workflow\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Identify the input.\u003c/strong> Record size, full hash, header, trainer/trailing data and physical PRG/CHR layout. Preserve the input unchanged. A legacy header can disagree with observed bank-switching code; keep the disagreement explicit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Choose an explicit range.\u003c/strong> General analysis accepts physical file offsets, length, CPU origin when known, and bank labels. File offset and CPU address are not interchangeable.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Separate code from data.\u003c/strong> Linear disassembly is a hypothesis. Check reset/NMI/IRQ vectors, reachable calls, indirect pointers, bank changes and data reads. Label uncertain ranges.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Find consumers.\u003c/strong> Trace PPU writes for backgrounds/palettes, OAM construction for sprites, APU writes for sound and RAM/state routines for behavior. The code consuming a record establishes its meaning.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Make a reproducible observation.\u003c/strong> Save an emulator checkpoint, replay scripted input or apply a reversible RAM experiment. Record starting state and stopping condition. Runtime experiments do not patch the ROM or authored project.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Retain evidence.\u003c/strong> Record bank + CPU address + physical offset, operand width/endian order, bounds, aliases, decoded meaning and unknown context. Export notes/report separately from project changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Implement only an established claim.\u003c/strong> A private decoder can return a read-only catalog before any visual import or writer exists. Unknown sources remain inspectable without a guessed adapter.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>For 8 KiB banks in a standard header/no-trainer example:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>fileOffset = 16 + physicalBank * 8192 + (cpuAddress - windowStart)\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>This formula requires a known mapped window. A trainer changes the payload start; NES 2.0 and other layouts need their actual container description. Read the container/layout owner instead of hard-coding 16 for an arbitrary game.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-6-worked-native-codec-lesson\">6. Worked native codec lesson\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Run the included exercise with Node:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node app/docs/native-codec-exercise.cjs\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>It defines an original teaching format, not a commercial ROM. A header points to fixed-capacity \u003ccode>[count, tile]\u003c/code> RLE data describing eight cells. Read its \u003ccode>decode\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>encode\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>exercise\u003c/code> functions in order.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The exercise demonstrates: strict bounds; a canonical grid; edits on a detached copy; run compression; capacity refusal; preservation of unrelated bytes; decoding the candidate; JSON save/reopen; and byte-identical unchanged derivative output. Extend it by adding a second shared view, then ensure editing a shared operand updates both views. Next add attributes as a separate record and reject mismatched ownership spans.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>In Punch-Out, the method becomes:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>punch-out-layout.js\u003c/code> independently interprets native PPU commands and records which output cells share literal operands.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>native-punch-out-resources.js\u003c/code> projects static backgrounds while retaining actual CHR-latch row context. The physical operand, not each visible cell, owns a native literal recipe.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>punch-out-arena.js\u003c/code> executes the original setup loader on a private bus, recovers two raw nametables, then projects a viewport using NT1 and scroll \u003ccode>$B0\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Each visible cell retains \u003ccode>cellSources\u003c/code>: its source nametable and native cell. This is the inverse map required by the writer.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>punch-out-arena-encoder.js\u003c/code> copies raw pages, applies supported tile/attribute changes back through that map, compresses into four streams, and stays inside the original 381-byte area plus four pointer words.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The encoder executes the original loader again on the candidate and compares all raw pages and the visible projection. Oversized or unrepresentable edits refuse with a useful error.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>punch-out-arena-writer.js\u003c/code> registers the encoder with the existing native build owner. It masks only \u003ccode>nt\u003c/code>/\u003ccode>attr\u003c/code> during projection-signature validation; CHR, colors, actors and unrelated settings remain protected.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The shared native owner composes literal recipes with arena writes, rejects overlapping claims, checks every changed byte and returns an artifact to the existing RuntimeCompiler.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>The compressor searches native literal, repeat, increment and repeated-sequence operations. Each page has two streams indexed by an 8-bit Y register: command bytes plus a terminating zero must fit before wrap. Minimal encoded byte cost and successful stream partitioning are different requirements; both must be checked. No general relocation/expansion is implemented.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The 2026-10-24 derivative correction adds a separate immutable identity for those supported arena write ranges. Recognition may ignore only declared mutable data and must still decode bounded valid programs. This does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> grant the literal brush ownership of the arena. Current stream pointers must stay in the owned area, terminate without Y wrap and initialize exactly both pages. Changes to header, code, Audio or CHR remain outside that identity.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-7-installing-another-decoder-or-encoder\">7. Installing another decoder or encoder\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Keep four responsibilities separate:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Inputs and outputs\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Project side effects\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Decoder/model\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source bytes \u2192 bounded records, geometry, provenance and gaps\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Projection/recovery service\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Validated records \u2192 canonical Scenes/poses/Animation and bindings\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Returns a prepared copy; does not commit UI state\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>UI\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Selection \u2192 preview \u2192 validated, guarded import\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>One existing snapshot/ProjectStore transaction\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Encoder\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Preserved bytes + supported edit intent \u2192 candidate bytes + diagnostics\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Returns a copy; never mutates source in place\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>Add your module at the correct place in \u003ccode>app/source-manifest.json\u003c/code>. Dependencies must execute before their consumers, and \u003ccode>composition.js\u003c/code> initializes the application after services/UI registration. Existing Punch-Out recovery wraps prior \u003ccode>NativeResourceProject.create\u003c/code> implementations; capture the preceding function instead of recursively calling the newly installed service.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a Scene writer the current registration contract is:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>// Sketch of the existing interface; provide real implementations and verified\n// source ranges for your adapter. This is not a universal encoder.\nowner.registerSceneEncoder({\n  id: 'your-verified-scene-format-v1',\n  matches(project, info) { /* identify supported project and Scene */ },\n  views(project, info) { /* [{id, nt: sourceNT, attr: sourceAttr}] */ },\n  spans(project, info) { /* [{offset, length}] exclusively owned ROM bytes */ },\n  encode(bytes, project, info) { /* {bytes: candidate, diagnostics: []} */ }\n});\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>views\u003c/code> controls supported-field signature comparison. It must not hide unrelated changed fields or accept Scenes from another source. \u003ccode>spans\u003c/code> declares writes, not generic permissions to modify the ROM. \u003ccode>encode\u003c/code> must preserve all preceding disjoint edits and reject anything it cannot represent. Dedicated Punch-Out art views now use explicitly bounded \u003ccode>chr\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pal\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sprPal\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>bgColor\u003c/code> masks through that same owner. Unknown fields and duplicate claims refuse; unrelated Scene state stays protected.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Before rebasing \u003ccode>projectionSha256\u003c/code> when adding recovered assets, validate the preceding project's native build. Otherwise unsupported user edits can accidentally become accepted baseline data. Re-sign only deliberate, validated source-derived additions. Do not hand-edit the hash to bypass a refusal.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-8-writing-6502-code-yourself\">8. Writing 6502 code yourself\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Use a separate authored project for custom assembly. Save the native recovery project first. In CODE choose the Minimal boot ROM or Scene viewer template; inspect the code before replacing any existing source. Use Assemble / Ctrl+Enter and review errors, symbols, origin and emitted bytes.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The assembler supports the implemented syntax including labels, \u003ccode>.org\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.byte\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>.word\u003c/code> and supported instructions/addressing modes. Consult \u003ccode>asm_assemble\u003c/code> for exact syntax. Overlapping addresses, out-of-range branches and unresolved operands must be fixed before output.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>A minimal boot template intentionally shows no graphics: it disables rendering, establishes a stack and loops, with reset/NMI/IRQ vectors. Add PPU initialization, graphics upload, NMI updates and controls deliberately. Use a complete live custom project build and test the emitted reset path.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>BuildGraph.backendFor\u003c/code> selects \u003ccode>native-engine\u003c/code> when a preserved native descriptor is present. Writing text into CODE does not turn that descriptor into a safe arbitrary native code patch. For authored custom output, use the matching viewer/custom settings; \u003ccode>packScenes=false\u003c/code> plus assembly and no conflicting gameplay/World mode selects unpacked custom output. Runtime Audio cannot be automatically injected into custom assembly. Detached compilation cannot invoke the live assembler.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For editing an existing game's code, assemble the replacement with your toolchain or the supported assembler, map CPU writes into the correct physical bank, reserve/verify owned space, and produce a separate patched ROM. Recover relocations, callers, vectors and timing as needed. Re-import for inspection; adding native adapter recognition/build support is a separate step. A disassembly listing alone is not complete linkable source.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-9-hex-editing-yourself\">9. Hex editing yourself\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Create a copy and record the exact original hash. Locate the physical offset using the active bank and container layout; verify expected original bytes before a patch. Change one field, export a distinct ROM and compare all changed ranges. Preserve your patch recipe separately so it can be reapplied and reviewed.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a one-byte observation, check every consumer: compressed operands can expand into many cells; pointers can be little-endian and bank-relative; palette bits can affect four neighboring tiles; CHR patterns can be shared across unrelated Scenes. A value used as both graphics and a latch control has runtime side effects.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Reopen the patched ROM in general analysis regardless of whether a specialized adapter recognizes it. If a protected code/CHR/Audio byte changed, the adapter may correctly stop recognizing the derivative. To support it as an editable native project, establish its new identity, verify its structures and add bounded writer support. Do not relax detection to a filename, mapper number or broad checksum mask.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For RAM hypotheses use the reversible experiment controls: checkpoint, apply, replay, restore or keep. Keeping changes only that emulator session. To make the behavior persistent, identify and patch the native initialization/update code or save format separately.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The read-only analysis pane, reversible RAM editor, narrow native command editor and external hex editor remain complementary workflows. None should silently replace or modify the current saved project.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-10-editing-the-standalone-html-or-modular-source\">10. Editing the standalone HTML or modular source\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-edit-the-html-directly\">Edit the HTML directly\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Copy the working HTML and save your project JSON. Open the HTML in a text/code editor.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Search for \u003ccode>NES FORGE DEVELOPER MAP\u003c/code>, then the relevant \u003ccode>NES FORGE MODULE:\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>NES FORGE CONNECTION:\u003c/code> marker. For unchanged legacy modules search the function names in the source index near the top.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Change one function, string, UI style or explicitly defined data structure. App JS/CSS lives in the HTML; native game assets appear only after your input is loaded. There is no hidden fixed bank of every game's assets in the application source.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep the script structure intact, avoid a literal closing script tag inside JavaScript data, and check DevTools Console after reopening the edited file. IIFE-local variables are scoped to their modules; the documented \u003ccode>Forge.services\u003c/code> APIs connect modules.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test fresh startup, existing project load, the changed workflow, Undo/Redo, save/reopen, native output and Audio where applicable. Keep the last working HTML alongside the candidate.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Port a successful direct change back to its source module before running the bundler. Bundling regenerates the HTML from source and overwrites direct-only changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>Changing an HTML function changes the application for future sessions. Changing a saved JSON field changes that project. Changing bytes in a rebuilt \u003ccode>.nes\u003c/code> changes the game. Pick the intended artifact rather than replacing all three.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-edit-modular-source\">Edit modular source\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>app/source-manifest.json\u003c/code> lists the ordered parts. \u003ccode>app/tools/bundle.py\u003c/code> concatenates them deterministically; it does not transpile, install packages or fetch runtime dependencies. Work in \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code>, then run from the repository root:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python3 app/tools/bundle.py\npython3 app/tools/bundle.py --check\nnode app/docs/native-codec-exercise.cjs\npython3 reviews/punch-out-developer-1/run_checks.py\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>The generated standalone app remains \u003ccode>app/mainline/NES_Forge_2.0.0-alpha.11.html\u003c/code>. The downloadable filename can differ while containing the identical bytes. \u003ccode>app/build-inputs.json\u003c/code> records input sizes/hashes and the generated identity. No server is required for the standalone editor; browser/device APIs still have their own capabilities.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Keep comments near decisions: source ownership, pointer mapping, private buses, work limits, stale-source checks, signature masking, alias updates, compression limits and failure behavior. Avoid comments that merely repeat a variable name. Historical metadata strings can be part of saved projection signatures; changing them may require a compatibility plan, not just a wording replacement.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-source-navigation\">Source navigation\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Responsibility\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Files below \u003ccode>app/src/\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Shell/CSS/static controls\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>shell-prefix.html\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>shell-suffix.html\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Original editor/assembler/undo and historical layers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/00-editor.js\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>legacy/29-release166.js\u003c/code>; follow later wrappers in manifest order\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Shared CPU instruction behavior\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/cpu6502-core.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Persistence and reference lookup\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/project-store.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/asset-registry.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Build planning and output\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/build-services.js\u003c/code>; runtime-specific compilers\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Project capabilities and applicable controls\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/project-capabilities.js\u003c/code> projects \u003ccode>BuildGraph.backendFor\u003c/code> and native owners; encoder registrations declare their own available scope. \u003ccode>ui/connected-workspace.js\u003c/code>, intake, Scene context and Area Library display it. \u003ccode>legacy/05-foundation.js\u003c/code> guards generated build-selection controls; \u003ccode>legacy/00-editor.js\u003c/code> and the shell guard native ROM configuration.\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Emulator/player\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>legacy/01-emulator.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/emulator-*\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/mmc2-emulator.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/mmc2.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Input and analysis\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/project-gateway.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/project-gateway-ui.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/re-analysis*\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>source/\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native resource ownership\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/rom-project.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/investigation-project.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/native-resource-project.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Scenes and navigation\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/scene-context.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/stage-context.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>services/area-library.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Punch-Out native formats\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-*.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>re/native-punch-out-resources.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Punch-Out asset projection/build registration\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-*.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native Audio catalog/playback/import\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/native-audio-*.js\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>audio/native-audio-library-ui.js\u003c/code>, driver-specific modules\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Authored Audio\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>audio/\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Worlds and gameplay\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>world/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>gameplay/\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Mashup/donor conversion\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>mashup/\u003c/code>, source asset import services\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Final initialization\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>composition.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-11-prove-the-workflow-not-just-the-helper\">11. Prove the workflow, not just the helper\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Begin with the current backend in \u003cstrong>Connected Workspace\u003c/strong>. Native tools lists the current project's registered writers, with explicit pickers for resources such as Punch-Out CHR banks and Glass Joe poses. Availability is separate from build acceptance: an unsupported copied edit can still block output. Native projects preserve their original engine configuration and do not use the generated Scene inclusion/order controls. Generated packed builds retain that selection; World output follows its saved dependencies, and unpacked custom assembly uses active CHR. No capability label changes a project signature or supplies an encoder.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>A decoder and its tests can share a wrong assumption. Earlier Punch-Out tests missed an import-path difference and mirrored rendering assumptions. Test original loaders and independent observations, and exercise the actual UI entry points.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For each supported asset/field, record:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Original-byte identity and revision; decoder bounds and explicit unresolved states.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Native/reference output agreement, including banks, palettes, flips and timing assumptions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Actual Open ROM / project \u2192 prepare \u2192 import path, not only direct \u003ccode>create()\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preview without mutation; stale preview rejection; one Undo transaction for import.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Edit \u2192 Undo \u2192 Redo \u2192 Save Project As \u2192 reopen in a fresh app instance.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unchanged build preserves original bytes; changed build modifies only owned ranges.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Original engine reload reproduces edits; derived-ROM re-import retains supported content.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared operand edits affect all aliases; unsupported/overflow/malformed edits refuse clearly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Audio driver/cues/audition/conversion/reopen and Scene-build preservation.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retained core tools and other verified projects; browser appearance, listening and play acceptance separately.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>For Punch-Out, preserve offscreen PPU fetch side effects. Its mapper selects CHR banks when particular pattern addresses are read, even if the fetched tile is outside the displayed viewport. The current mapper-9 renderer retains 34 background pattern fetches per scanline; clipping output pixels must not remove bus activity. The prior visual corruption also occurred with the untouched ROM, which separated the player defect from the arena writer.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>This correction does not establish a cycle-accurate NES PPU. Full fights, alternate states, sprite/prefetch ordering, browser listening, controls and hardware remain separate acceptance work. MMC2 runtime capture is still explicitly unavailable.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-current-verification-commands\">Current verification commands\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>Public synthetic/retained suites and the tutorial exercise:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>python3 reviews/punch-out-developer-1/run_checks.py\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>Supplied-input workflow checks (local ROM directory required; no game assets are distributed):\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node reviews/punch-out-developer-1/verify_reimport.cjs /path/to/private-roms\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>Additional retained original-loader/build and player comparison scripts live in \u003ccode>reviews/punch-out-arena-runtime-1/\u003c/code>. Their reports are dated checkpoints, not automatic evidence for later edits. The restored snapshot lacks some historical full-release suite scripts; the current available gate must not be described as the complete historical release gate.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-12-debugging-and-extending-the-project\">12. Debugging and extending the project\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Inspect first\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>What the result tells you\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>CHR sheet displayed as a scene\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Selected context, layout decoder, bank/palette projection\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Patterns may be recovered while placement is missing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Recognized original becomes generic after rebuild\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Immutable identity ranges and current command validity\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Output may be valid but outside the adapter's admitted derivative format\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Correct intro but missing ring\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Scene catalog vs arena source programs/scroll\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Different runtime contexts must be assembled separately\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Fighter garbled after a size change\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Native sprite-size and tile-stride state, prior OAM and CHR\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Independent frame decoding may have discarded required context\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Scene edit saves but build refuses\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Projection signature and registered encoder scope\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Saving is working; an inverse writer is missing or edit is unsupported\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Native load reads correct bytes but live picture is wrong\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Untouched ROM, PPU bank/latch/scroll timing\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The player can be the defect rather than the writer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Audio missing after reopen\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Investigation restore lifecycle, input bytes, profile identity and catalog\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Check shared loading path before inventing another driver\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Hex patch loses recognition\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Protected ranges and revised data/control flow\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Investigate the new revision; do not silently weaken recognition\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Asset changes disappear after selection\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Active \u003ccode>S\u003c/code> working buffers vs Scene serialization\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Update through existing owners and capture/commit consistently\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Direct HTML changes disappear\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source module vs generated output\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Port the change into \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code> before rebundling\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>A useful contribution includes a bounded claim, source references, readable module comments, owned data ranges, failure behavior, lifecycle/Audio evidence and a clear list of remaining gaps. Another developer should be able to replace the decoder, use an external toolchain, or patch a separate ROM without losing access to the rest of NES Forge.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-13-concrete-asset-edits-from-javascript-or-html\">13. Concrete asset edits from JavaScript or HTML\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>To make a deliberate one-time project change from DevTools, use a captured copy and the same transaction as the recovery UI. This example paints an existing tile in the selected Scene. Use it on an authored Scene or the supported recovered arena, with a valid tile number for that Scene:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>const store = Forge.services.ProjectStore;\nconst project = store.capture();\nconst scene = project.scenes[project.activeScene];\nconst x = 12, y = 19;\nscene.nt[y * 32 + x] = 36;\nconst prepared = store.prepareLoad(project);\nif (snapshot() === false) throw Error('History could not retain the current project');\nstore.commit(prepared, {sync: true, intent: 'manual-scene-tile'});\nnotify('manual-scene-tile');\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>The transaction preserves ordinary Undo/Redo and lets ProjectStore normalize/validate data. Save Project As to make the edit portable. A native recipe-owned intro page may refuse this direct Scene edit because its physical recipe must be changed through the native brush; the arena has its own Scene encoder. This example grants no new native writer capability.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The same detached-copy transaction can deliberately change a pixel (\u003ccode>scene.chr[tile][y][x] = colorIndex\u003c/code>), a palette color (\u003ccode>scene.pal[group][slot] = nesColor\u003c/code>), a sprite piece, or an animation duration, provided the relevant validator accepts the value and all IDs/references remain valid. Native output can still refuse when those fields lack writers. For a multi-asset change, prepare and commit the full project once instead of repeatedly mutating live arrays.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To bake an application change into HTML, edit the owner function rather than pasting project data into a random global. Examples:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Intended change\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Edit location / method\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Test\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change button text/help\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Search the control ID and its \u003ccode>textContent\u003c/code> or shell markup\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Fresh load and the workflow that updates that label again\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change editor appearance\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Relevant CSS in the shell or module's generated style\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Desktop/narrow widths, scrolling and controls\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change a default authored asset\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Find its factory such as \u003ccode>makeScene\u003c/code> or the relevant model defaults; distinguish later wrappers\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>New authored project plus loading an older saved project\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change native scene projection\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>The decoder's placement/palette/bank mapping; preserve provenance and inverse map\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Independent original-loader/render comparisons and native edit rebuild\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Add a recovered asset tab connection\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Recovery service + explicit UI materialization through ProjectStore\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source selection, stale preview, Undo/Redo and save/reopen\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change an animation recovery rule\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>State decoder plus canonical projection, not only the preview renderer\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Full observed update sequence, durations and source context\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change native build behavior\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing owner/registered encoder and its exclusive write ranges\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Original-engine decode, protected bytes and derivative re-import\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Change sound recognition/playback\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Driver identity/catalog or NativeAudioMachine as appropriate\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Every affected cue, native register timing, cancellation and reopening\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>Patch game bytes\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Separate copied ROM plus documented physical patch ranges\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Binary diff and original-engine run; revised adapter identity if needed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>For an HTML-only experiment, put the change inside the relevant existing module closure or at a documented service extension point. Simply appending code after the closing script does not execute it as JavaScript; installing code after application startup can also miss required registrations. Port the final change into the corresponding source module and manifest order.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Do not paste a private source-bearing project into the application template. Ordinary asset exports/imports and saved JSON provide portable asset work; the application remains reusable across projects.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-14-glass-joe-fight-references-mac-referee-and-hud\">14. Glass Joe fight references: Mac, referee and HUD\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The next recovery pass adds a \u003cstrong>Glass Joe fight reference \u00b7 Little Mac / referee / HUD\u003c/strong> section inside \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong>. Open the supported original or an existing recovered project, click \u003cstrong>Recovered content\u003c/strong>, then expand that section.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a \u003cstrong>Little Mac command\u003c/strong> and a \u003cstrong>Referee / speech command\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Preview pose composition\u003c/strong>. There are 98 Mac and 47 extended referee/speech command descriptors. These are source commands: some clear cells, draw a fragment or update a count/speech bubble. They are not all self-contained full poses.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The default combines Mac command 0, referee command 144, and Glass Joe source pose 0. The opponent starts at the source configuration's opening position near the upper right; this is a setup/reference composition, not a simulated active fight. Full initial health, hearts, stars, score and clock come from the original HUD routines.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a \u003cstrong>Native player state\u003c/strong>, set \u003cstrong>Native updates\u003c/strong> (1\u2013600), and use \u003cstrong>Trace player / referee state\u003c/strong>. The 23 available state entries produce bounded zero-input paths. Pick an \u003cstrong>Observed excerpt frame\u003c/strong> to inspect its duration, fine scroll and graphics-bank context. A state can wait for input or another game system; \u003ccode>update-limit\u003c/code> means the excerpt is bounded, not that every branch has been recovered.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Open selected fight reference\u003c/strong> adds the selected composition/frame as an ordinary Scene in one Undo transaction. Save Project As and reopen to preserve its native bitplanes, per-scanline tile/palette placement, separate opponent OAM and source metadata. This uses the existing raster representation; it does not turn the source into a screenshot overlay or a new animation schema.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Edit native arena layout\u003c/strong> returns to the existing native arena Scene and writer. Paint supported tiles/attributes there, build, then make a new reference to inspect the current encoded arena beneath the characters. Changing the reference copy's CHR, palette, sprites or background remains savable but blocks native builds because those fields have no reference encoder.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Open source Audio\u003c/strong> retains the original Audio library. Observed state sound requests remain source evidence; they do not become runtime bindings or native Audio edits automatically.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Browse all CHR banks\u003c/strong>, from a fight reference, first selects the source arena and then opens the existing raw bank browser. The reference's raster atlas and physical CHR banks remain separate views of source graphics.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>The implementation is additive: \u003ccode>re/punch-out-fight.js\u003c/code> supplies a bounded private CPU/PPU bus and source projection; \u003ccode>services/punch-out-fight-recovery.js\u003c/code> validates and prepares canonical additions; \u003ccode>re/punch-out-fight-ui.js\u003c/code> owns detached preview and guarded ProjectStore transactions. All 168 preceding application inputs remain byte-identical, in their original order.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Why background commands? Punch-Out draws Little Mac and the referee through bank-11 nametable streams. Their placement, partial updates and CHR-bank changes are different from the opponents' sprite/metasprite system. Recovery keeps those differences. The projector retains 34 background fetches per scanline, including offscreen latch controls, and the source OAM order/scanline limit. Fine scrolling is represented by raster runs rather than flattened into an inaccurate 8\u00d78 grid.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Verification compares every catalogued command and each observed state update with the original routines through the actual emulator CPU/mapper/PPU bus. Representative compositions and fine-scroll frames also compare background and sprite composite pixels. These checks establish the declared source reference and projection; they do not establish original NMI transfer arbitration, a complete concurrent fight, cycle-accurate hardware behavior or native Mac/referee animation or Audio writers. Dedicated physical CHR/fight-palette writing is a separate path below.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>node reviews/punch-out-fight-context-1/verify_fight.cjs /path/to/private-roms\nnode reviews/punch-out-fight-context-1/verify_lifecycle.cjs /path/to/private-roms\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>Source ROMs, source-bearing projects and decoded screenshots stay outside the repository and review deliverables. Native writing of these reference copies and complete gameplay-context recovery remain separate work; dedicated physical art/color editing is described below.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-15-dedicated-native-artwork-and-concurrent-playback\">15. Dedicated native artwork and concurrent playback\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The current combined candidate adds dedicated \u003cstrong>Native artwork \u00b7 CHR banks and fight palettes\u003c/strong> editing and \u003cstrong>Open Glass Joe native replay\u003c/strong>. Read sections 13\u201315 of \u003ccode>projects/PUNCH_OUT.md\u003c/code> for the complete current steps, native ownership ranges, color-flag preservation, original-engine sequence, reversible knockdown/count/round experiments and adopted Retro Workstation controls. The earlier reference-copy limitations in this guide still apply to those copies; native writes belong to the dedicated physical art views.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-art-project.js\u003c/code> registers physical CHR/color field ownership with NativeResourceProject. The source remains preserved, unknown fields remain protected and byte spans are disjoint from layout recipes. \u003ccode>services/punch-out-replay.js\u003c/code> uses the existing EmulatorBridge/Timeline, with real input, owned checkpoints and explicitly recorded temporary RAM seeds. It does not introduce a second CPU or enable MMC2 runtime Scene capture.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The supplied Resources, Connected Workspace, metasprite context and Retro Workstation modules are now source-manifest inputs under \u003ccode>src/ui/\u003c/code>. Presentation preferences remain outside canonical projects. The full asset map, independent RE, code/hex paths, standalone application edits and separate authored-game workflow remain available alongside these title-specific extensions.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-native-glass-joe-full-pose-position-extension\">Native Glass Joe full-pose position extension\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>Section 16 of \u003ccode>projects/PUNCH_OUT.md\u003c/code> adds a dedicated native position view for each of Glass Joe's 30 full poses. The frame and source-art Scene are ordinary canonical assets. The signed native X/Y operands are derived from uniform piece displacement minus the frame origin, so no hidden placement recipe or alternate asset store is required. Individual pieces, shared geometry, position-less incremental records and animation remain separate writing problems. Five explicit incremental positions extend this method below.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ctable>\n\u003cthead>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003cth>Owner\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Responsibility\u003c/th>\n  \u003cth>Connection\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\n\u003ctbody>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Pure pointer/position codec; validates the output through the original CPU shape reader\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Source bytes \u2192 canonical placement \u2192 two signed native bytes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>services/punch-out-pose-project.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Dedicated Scene/Metasprite ownership and bounded encoding\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>NativeResourceProject's existing encoder registry, projection gate and disjoint-span checks\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n  \u003ctd>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose-ui.js\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Pose selection, native X/Y controls, one Undo transaction, build check and contextual artwork shortcuts\u003c/td>\n  \u003ctd>Existing Metasprites, CHR Sheet, native artwork UI, ProjectStore, history and RuntimeCompiler\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\n\u003c/table>\n\n\u003cp>The native resource gate now accepts an optional \u003ccode>metaspriteViews\u003c/code> callback on a registered encoder. It compares declared baseline origin/piece positions while retaining every unrelated project field in the signature. The encoder separately proves uniform movement, unchanged piece identities/order/tiles/flags, signed-byte range and exact owned-byte writes. Masking a recognition range or registering a projection never grants permission to write another resource. With no native pose view present, the preceding projection and output remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For standalone HTML work, change these authoritative modules and re-bundle. For independent hex work, the title lesson identifies the actual position operands and validation steps. Moving a visual pose leaves the original damage/attack/collision code in place, so check alignment in the running game. Native pose editing is scoped to whole-pose placement; it is not complete fighter or animation recovery.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-native-idle-and-incremental-position-extension\">Native idle and incremental position extension\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>Section 17 of \u003ccode>projects/PUNCH_OUT.md\u003c/code>, included in the offline handbook, covers five additional explicit X/Y pairs, the grouped \u003ccode>$C0/$C2/$C4\u003c/code> idle workflow, file offsets, external hex editing and direct HTML/source changes. The existing codec now calls \u003ccode>PunchOutStates\u003c/code> to preserve preceding OAM and the observed moving anchor; reading incremental frames independently would lose that context. All 30 earlier full-pose views retain their original identities and source ownership.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The same native encoder owns these pairs through ordinary Metasprites. Incremental views use a separate \u003ccode>punchOutIncrementalPose\u003c/code> marker so older full-pose-only readers preserve the ordinary assets and refuse unsupported native building. The idle helper adds missing derived views and changes three existing canonical frames in one guarded project transaction. It preserves their current relative displacement and original timing; it does not create a second animation/runtime store.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For the \u003ccode>$C0\u003c/code> family, source routine \u003ccode>$D043\u003c/code> derives priority from native OAM Y at the \u003ccode>$50\u003c/code> divider. \u003ccode>moveFrame\u003c/code> updates canonical \u003ccode>behind\u003c/code> flags to match that consequence of position. The encoder independently checks this exact derivation; manual flag changes still refuse. Source attribute streams, flags, pointers and code remain protected. Full-pose and other incremental attribute handling keep their earlier rules.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Derivative recognition adds exactly ten physical operand bytes; it does not widen generic recipe writes. Existing native art/palette and arena owners still encode separate spans. Tests check every earlier full pose, all five new records, observed primary-state contexts, priority consequences, one-step grouped Undo/Redo, save/reopen, older-reader refusal, composed native output and real exported/reimported idle playback. The title lesson keeps unobserved paths and complete-fight/animation/Audio writing limits explicit.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch3 id=\"general-pose-and-chr-navigation\">Pose and CHR navigation\u003c/h3>\n\n\u003cp>The native pose picker is expanded above the source-reference preview in Recovered content. The native position panel links to \u003cstrong>View pose sprite tiles\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Edit native sprite artwork\u003c/strong>. Entering CHR Sheet with an owned pose Scene selects its sprite buffer; the sheet's contextual strip also exposes the source-verified physical bank and a return to position editing. Opening a dedicated physical CHR view selects its background buffer, which is the registered pixel writer. A deliberate bank toggle still works in the current sheet.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The reference sprite buffer and the physical writer remain separate existing assets. Navigation does not copy pixels, replace snapshots, re-sign unsupported work, patch source or create an alternate encoder. \u003ccode>PunchOutPoseProject.describe\u003c/code> returns the verified \u003ccode>chrBank4KiB\u003c/code> for UI routing. Existing native validation still runs before a physical view can be added/selected; copied-reference pixel changes refuse native building and cannot be adopted as a new baseline by following the shortcut. See the Punch-Out handbook's section 17 for the click-by-click path and save/build/reimport exercise.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-16-save-and-restore-editor-context-without-changing-the-game\">16. Save and restore editor context without changing the game\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Portable \u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>, browser project saves, recovery and Export's \u003ccode>project.json\u003c/code> include optional browsing preferences. A fresh open can return to the same Metasprite and piece, core editor tab, selected World, CHR buffer/tile and used-pose view. The active Scene and selected pose remain independent when their declared source references differ. Choosing another World in the editor does not change \u003ccode>worldRuntime.worldId\u003c/code> or its spawn.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For independent JSON or HTML work, the additional metadata has this shape:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"editorView\": {\n    \"version\": 1,\n    \"worldId\": \"an-existing-world-id\",\n    \"metaspriteId\": \"an-existing-pose-id\",\n    \"pieceId\": \"a-piece-in-that-pose\",\n    \"tab\": \"chr\",\n    \"chrUsage\": \"pose\"\n  }\n}\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\n\n\u003cp>These are existing asset IDs and a few bounded view values, not new asset records. \u003ccode>chrUsage\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>pose\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>whole\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>tab\u003c/code> must name an available core editor. Existing top-level \u003ccode>activeScene\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>chrBank\u003c/code> and tile/Animation selections retain their current formats. A deliberately empty \u003ccode>metaspriteId\u003c/code> preserves an unselected composer. A stale or invalid ID does not bind a different Scene or regenerate assets. With no valid saved pose, a unique pose using the active Scene can be selected; several matches produce an explicit choice, and no matching pose leaves the composer unselected.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ProjectStore.capture({includeEditorView:true})\u003c/code> includes this block for saves. Default \u003ccode>capture()\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>serialize()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>authoringData()\u003c/code> keep the existing build/authoring boundaries; the latter removes view metadata. Native signatures, source bytes, encoder spans and raw recipe hashing rules are unchanged. Ordinary projects remain schema 12; existing native formats and the reader ceiling of 16 stay intact. An older reader may discard optional browsing preferences safely. Unknown future preference versions can retain the old World hint but do not introduce new navigation rules.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The implementation uses the existing owners:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>services/editor-view.js\u003c/code> reads a whitelist of bounded hints, captures current UI IDs and restores them through the existing Metasprite/World/CHR editors. It owns no asset store, encoder or persistent database.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>services/project-store.js\u003c/code> owns save/load/history and includes the block only at explicit save boundaries. Validation of canonical project data still runs before UI state is touched.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>legacy/13-metasprites.js\u003c/code> exposes \u003ccode>restoreSelection\u003c/code> for UI IDs and an explicit chooser when fallback would be ambiguous. It adds no pieces, Scene OAM or source references.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>re/punch-out-pose-ui.js\u003c/code> can resolve a native CHR return shortcut from the restored canonical pose, instead of requiring a previous click history in the same document.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>To change this behavior in the standalone HTML, save a copy and locate those module comments. To maintain source, edit their files under \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, rebuild the native/root and hosted outputs, and check them. Do not put view preferences into a native projection signature or refresh copied source pixels merely to align a preview. Verify fresh-document reopening, intentional mismatches, old/malformed hints, Undo/Redo, all save transports and byte-identical output. Optional metadata does not save emulator RAM, resume playback or establish a recovered runtime binding.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-17-preview-the-actual-resource-and-check-the-actual-build\">17. Preview the actual resource and check the actual build\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>A consistent preview needs explicit source, palette, composition and output scope. Resources Scene previews use \u003ccode>forgeCapture.scenePixels\u003c/code> for background plus active Scene OAM, including captured raster states. Pose previews use the canonical Metasprite compositor. CHR Resources uses the existing \u003ccode>forgeCHR\u003c/code> appearance resolver: current color mode/appearance for the displayed buffer, automatic source usage when browsing a different buffer, transparent sprite index 0, and the correct page of a capture atlas. These views do not create Scene placements or change assets.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Supported native Glass Joe position views also offer \u003cstrong>Preview current native output\u003c/strong>. It captures the project through ProjectStore, calls the same live BuildGraph/RuntimeCompiler entry as Build &amp; Run, which captures a detached source, and decodes the supported pose from the exact resulting ROM. The pose compositor receives detached encoded pixels/colors; the source snapshot remains untouched. A failed compile produces a refusal, not an original-ROM fallback. Incremental preview is limited to the declared state path, with no claim of a live fight or complete game palette/priority behavior.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Connected Workspace \u2192 Project Health \u2192 Check current ROM build\u003c/strong> runs the real current backend without committing the result, downloading or changing emulator state. Relationship counts remain a separate diagnostic. A successful result is tied to the captured input and becomes stale after edits; it does not certify gameplay. Code preflight and failed builds surface the same available structured native refusal details.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To repair a refusal, read its backend message, resource ID and confirmed field path, then use \u003cstrong>Inspect this resource\u003c/strong>. Save unsupported work before ordinary Undo. Make the intended change through the declared writer and recheck. Unsupported assets remain savable. A signature mismatch alone cannot locate every edit: native owners name a source-copy field only when a reproducible detached candidate restores the existing saved projection hash. Unproven differences stay explicitly unlocalized. Do not replace a signature or refresh a copied snapshot to make a refused project appear valid.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Developer entry points:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>services/resource-preview.js\u003c/code> supplies explicit context to the existing Scene/CHR/pose renderers and the native compiler. It stores no assets or output cache.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>legacy/04-saveas-chr.js\u003c/code> keeps CHR usage/palette rules in one resolver, including captured palettes and selected-pose contexts. \u003ccode>legacy/13-metasprites.js\u003c/code> accepts an explicit detached draw source.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>services/build-diagnostics.js\u003c/code> carries error text/context into UI. \u003ccode>services/build-services.js\u003c/code> retains build routing and compilation; \u003ccode>checkCurrent\u003c/code> invokes those owners without commit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>services/native-resource-project.js\u003c/code> still owns signatures, recipes and write spans. Registered title owners can expose reproducible diagnostic views; these do not mask fields or authorize edits. Zelda II terrain budget and native screen layout errors attach their actual World/Scene context at the refusing encoder.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>ui/resource-relationships.js\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>ui/connected-workspace.js\u003c/code> render preview scope and compiler results. The results are disposable and are not included in project schema or persistence.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>For an HTML-only experiment, copy the complete standalone HTML and search for these module comments or \u003ccode>fcw-check-build\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>frx-native-output\u003c/code>. For maintained work, edit \u003ccode>app/src\u003c/code>, update both manifests only if adding a module, and regenerate the native/root and optional hosted outputs. Keep one shared source and use the existing project lifecycle. Independent RE, assembly and external hex workflows continue to use explicit file/CPU/PPU addresses and verified diffs; a preview relationship does not grant byte ownership.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Verification should compare actual canvas pixels (not just the existence of a canvas), include live and independently browsed Scenes, raster palettes, sprite transparency/flips/priority, CHR preview modes and atlas pages. Native cases must compare snapshot versus compiled output, preserve canonical assets/signatures, exercise real refusals and repair navigation, and recheck save/reopen and target playback. See the Punch-Out chapter section 21 for a complete worked sequence.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"general-18-current-independent-and-detached-sources-in-app-lessons\">18. Current, independent and detached sources; in-app lessons\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Forge can hold a project, an inspected ROM, a native Audio catalog, an emulator ROM and a multi-file source workspace at the same time. They do not become one editable object because their names or pixels resemble one another.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Current project source\u003c/strong> means matching declared/preserved source identity with a valid owning session. It is not a claim that every recovered field has an encoder. \u003cstrong>Independent source\u003c/strong> means an explicitly chosen inspection separate from that project. \u003cstrong>Detached source\u003c/strong> means an earlier project/source guard has expired, even if a hash happens to match. \u003cstrong>Inspected project file\u003c/strong> identifies a saved JSON input rather than ROM bytes. The independent multi-file source workspace keeps its existing separate build/export path.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Project intake expires after its guarded project snapshot changes. Its old report remains readable, but actions disable and the panel collapses with an explicit detached-input label; expand it to review the retained report. Prepare/import previews also invalidate visibly after project replacement or relevant edits. Audio retains a previous catalog as evidence with stale operations disabled and any staged import cancelled. \u003cstrong>Continue independent Audio inspection\u003c/strong> explicitly reopens those exact retained bytes through the original loader; it does not modify the current project or emulator. \u003cstrong>Use current project source\u003c/strong> instead reads the active project's preserved ROM through that same loader and guards its source/epoch without loading the emulator. A new conversion preview and the existing project/source guards are still required before tracker import. \u003ccode>NativeAudioLibraryUI.getState().isCurrent\u003c/code> continues to describe the source session; \u003ccode>sourceRole\u003c/code> explains its relationship to the project.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Independent general RE and multi-file source remain available across project replacement. Their labels explain that distinction rather than deleting deliberate independent work. A selected-but-unopened RE file leaves the previous inspection visibly detached until a source is opened. Source labels are UI evidence only: they do not change schema, native signatures, recognition, recipes, byte ownership, asset references or runtime rules.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Project lessons\u003c/strong> opens the maintained offline chapters within \u003cstrong>TECH DOCS\u003c/strong>. The initial section follows the selected project/asset, including independently selected poses. Resources offers \u003cstrong>Lesson for this resource\u003c/strong>; Audio offers \u003cstrong>Lesson for this Audio source\u003c/strong>; intake offers \u003cstrong>Lesson for inspected input\u003c/strong>. Every chapter remains manually selectable, including source-page methods, Zelda II terrain/locations, Ninja source/copies, other title/Audio notes, the developer guide, verification requirements and synthetic exercise. The original Tech Docs and authoring reference remain below the reader. Historical acceptance notes retain their own scope; a chapter's presence does not certify complete title recovery.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>To extend this in source or HTML:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Edit/add the title Markdown under \u003ccode>app/docs/projects\u003c/code>. Describe actual supported fields, source formats, shared consumers, limits, Audio, save/reopen/build and independent RE/ASM/hex/application paths.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a chapter to \u003ccode>app/tools/render_project_tutorials.py\u003c/code> when needed. It renders the standalone handbook and \u003ccode>app/src/docs/project-lessons-data.js\u003c/code> from the same Markdown/anchors, escaping script delimiters. \u003ccode>--check\u003c/code> detects drift. The generated data contains documentation only, with source paths and hashes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a known profile/adapter mapping in \u003ccode>ui/project-lessons.js\u003c/code>. Choose by installed identifiers/source evidence, never by filename. Resolve the exact selected resource and keep current output scope separate from an independent source's lesson.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep \u003ccode>services/source-context.js\u003c/code> read-only. The UI consumes existing ProjectStore epoch/snapshot and source callbacks; it must not weaken or replace those guards. \u003ccode>InvestigationUI.projectChanged\u003c/code> and existing edit/UI events refresh presentation. Stale inputs require fresh inspection, not a rewritten signature.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Regenerate the native/root and optional hosted outputs from the shared manifests. Test source switching, authored replacement, delayed reads, cancelled/stale imports, explicit independent continuation, all lesson links/sections, source/workspace preservation, save/reopen and unchanged compiled bytes.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>For a quick HTML-only experiment, copy the standalone HTML and locate those module comments or \u003ccode>projectLessonsOpen\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nativeAudioIndependent\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>projectGatewayPanel\u003c/code>. To change lesson text durably, edit Markdown and regenerate rather than maintaining a second HTML tutorial copy. Reading a lesson never imports the shown example or changes native game rules.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/NATIVE_RECOVERY_TUTORIAL.md", "sourceSha256": "a31639a1f8b5deb6d603c5e2272b6a64a023ca623330678c9d634b98fdd5f3a7"}, {"id": "template", "title": "Template for the next title", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"template-project-tutorial-template\">Project tutorial template\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Copy this structure for a newly supported title. Replace the instructions with actual observed steps and source-backed facts; do not publish an unfilled template as a verified title tutorial. The template is reusable for games with different methods. It does not prescribe a common game data format.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-1-outcome-and-exact-input\">1. Outcome and exact input\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>State one concrete learner outcome and the scope of the checkpoint. Record title, region/revision, byte length, source SHA-256, mapper/container, application SHA-256, source commit and relevant adapter IDs. List admitted derivatives separately. Explain how the learner obtains and keeps their own input. Do not embed source-bearing projects or game payloads in the tutorial.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Name each deliverable: the standalone application HTML, project JSON, native rebuilt ROM, independent patch/notes, and any separate authored ROM. Explain what is preserved in each and how to reopen it. Record the exact tested browser/tool/runtime versions when they are known; do not infer them.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-2-first-successful-edit\">2. First successful edit\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Give numbered steps from a fresh app, using actual button labels and selectors. Include the source area/page/state, coordinate convention, valid replacement ID, expected visual change, and any aliases it also changes. Identify a route or reproducible setup for seeing it in the original engine. If that route has not been verified, say so.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Require the learner to check an unchanged native build first, then edit \u2192 Undo \u2192 Redo \u2192 Save Project As \u2192 reopen \u2192 build \u2192 reimport \u2192 inspect/play. Explain whether a reference image is derived from setup code, a bounded trace or actual live capture. Each result needs the right claim.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-3-every-asset-and-its-owner\">3. Every asset and its owner\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Use a table with columns: domain, source format/ranges, runtime context, canonical record/editor, supported edits, native writer, evidence and gaps. Include at least:\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Background/sprite CHR, raw versus assembled views, graphics loads and bank selection.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tile/metatile placement, attributes, color palettes, scrolling and raster effects.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sprite placement/priority, metasprites, animations, duration units and state dependencies.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collision, actors/AI, behavior, hitboxes, entities, items and persistence.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>World layout, scene links, doors/entrances/exits, camera and travel rules.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Menus, text, HUD, alternate states and transitions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Code, RAM, pointers, executable routines and source/disassembly distinctions.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Music, SFX, instruments/envelopes, sample banks, request routing and event links.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>Use explicit statuses such as source inspection only, copied editable asset, supported native field, or unresolved. A blank cell cannot stand for support. Document the actual shared-write owner and all affected views.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-4-source-format-lesson\">4. Source format lesson\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Show the relevant container and bank windows, a verified CPU-address-to-file-offset example, endianness and pointer base. Explain compression, termination, budgets, inherited state, clipping and aliases. Separate physical tile IDs from any projected atlas indices.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Show the original loader's inputs and outputs. Explain how an independent comparison avoids reproducing the decoder's own mistake. Include a small synthetic codec exercise with no commercial asset bytes. Document fixed-capacity refusal, malformed input, unsupported state and conflicting-alias behavior.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-5-visual-editing-recipes\">5. Visual editing recipes\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Write a separate worked recipe for each supported domain. Each needs prerequisites, actual control labels, an expected result, save/reopen, build path, original-engine check and failure recovery. Describe which tabs are populated on intake and which require an explicit materialization action. Never send a learner to an empty tab without explaining why.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a blocked domain, explain what can be inspected or saved, why native rebuilding refuses, and what decoder/writer work is still needed. Explain how to preserve that experiment separately and return to a native-buildable baseline.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-6-audio-recipe\">6. Audio recipe\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Record the exact driver/profile, catalog counts by cue type, valid controls, default selection, source banks and request meaning. Give a native audition example and an editable-conversion example when available. Show the declared timing window, representability limits, import effect on order/tempo, Undo/Redo and reopening.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Check Audio from the rebuilt native Scene output as well. Explain whether authored tracker changes have a native writer. A finite score or APU trace does not establish lossless music recovery, game event bindings or native Audio writing. Missing Audio remains an explicit open item under \u003ccode>reviews/PROJECT_VERIFICATION.md\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-7-independent-re-assembly-and-external-hex-work\">7. Independent RE, assembly and external hex work\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Give a workflow that does not require Forge automation: record identities, choose an external debugger/disassembler/toolchain, map the active bank, follow source consumers, form a hypothesis and compare its outputs. Refer to capabilities rather than recommending unverified tool versions.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a worked hex patch, record exact source hash, physical offset, expected original bytes, new bytes and every affected consumer. Write a new output, compare all changed ranges and test the original engine. Different code/palette/CHR edits may require new recognition; never disable identity checks merely to accept them.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For code edits, explain entry/callers, CPU versus file addresses, reserved space, relocations, vectors, stack, RAM and timing. The CODE editor in a preserved native project is not an unrestricted native code injector. Disassembly is not automatically complete rebuildable assembly.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-8-modify-the-app-and-produce-standalone-html\">8. Modify the app and produce standalone HTML\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>List exact source modules and their contracts, public APIs, manifest order, HTML module markers and transaction boundaries. Explain how UI preview stays detached, how stale materialization is rejected, how Undo/Redo and save/reopen work, and how the writer restricts bytes.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Provide deterministic bundle/check commands and a small worked application modification. Explain that direct HTML edits need to be ported back to modular source before regeneration. Verify the application contains no embedded private game/project data.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-9-make-a-separate-standalone-game\">9. Make a separate standalone game\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Name the intended output: a rebuilt original-engine ROM, a newly authored Forge ROM, or a customized Forge HTML. They are separate exercises.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For a new authored ROM, begin with a separate authored project; import only supported asset types, preserve stable IDs/references, select a supported build backend, and implement missing gameplay deliberately. List retained semantics and adaptations. Do not imply that deleting \u003ccode>nativeGame\u003c/code> converts an arbitrary imported game into an authored one. Validate the separate ROM in another emulator and explain the outstanding controller/hardware tests.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"template-10-evidence-troubleshooting-and-learner-checklist\">10. Evidence, troubleshooting and learner checklist\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Link source files, commands, reports and owner acceptance. Record report identities and distinguish historical evidence from checks rerun for this checkpoint. Include likely symptoms, diagnosis and recovery. Add checks for malformed input, overflow, aliases, protected bytes, stale preview, one Undo transaction, persistence and retained features.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>End with the learner's concrete outputs and remaining work, including unrecovered domains and unsupported writers. Keep a change log of documentation scope. Update this chapter whenever the title's method changes; do not call its tutorial finished because the importer recognizes a filename.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "app/docs/projects/PROJECT_TUTORIAL_TEMPLATE.md", "sourceSha256": "3beee5eefaa58a700cc85304928b92e5160defdcc790610de459d45def8fd2cf"}, {"id": "metroid", "title": "Metroid room workflow", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"metroid-metroid-focused-verification-checkpoint\">Metroid \u2014 focused verification checkpoint\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>The recovered starting HTML is preserved. The earlier Mega Man implementation is retained. Work in this checkpoint is bounded to Metroid; the remaining batch stays queued until owner review.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"metroid-what-changed\">What changed\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Metroid (U), exact source SHA-256 \u003ccode>c5eea06e1e1128b576bd789f1a4f63bb154d6d31579c2f319382fb77a72d34a6\u003c/code>, now imports 193 source room slots across five areas, backed by 191 physical room records. Rooms display assembled backgrounds using native structures, initial area graphics, original tile placement and native palette attributes. These are source-table slots, not a reconstructed world itinerary.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The renderer retains blank tile 255, native structure-row offsets and clipping, and ordered attribute updates. A structure using the room's default palette leaves previous overlapping attribute bits intact. All 193 slots have source-backed initial background graphics; none use a raw CHR sheet as their room layout.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The Scene tab exposes \u003cstrong>NATIVE ROOM STRUCTURES\u003c/strong>. Replace an existing room object's structure while retaining that object's position, palette and place in the draw order. The recipe changes only the object's original structure-index byte. Source records shared by two room slots remain one owner. A replacement may change the visible footprint, collision behavior or accessibility through the native engine; the editor does not prove that the resulting room is playable.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The existing canonical Scene/ProjectStore/history/native-build workflow remains authoritative. No second editable stage store is introduced. Ordinary projects remain schema 12; portable native-resource projects remain schema 16. Readers without this adapter reject it rather than drop the recipe.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"metroid-try-this-first\">Try this first\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Open \u003ccode>NES_Forge.html\u003c/code> in a desktop browser. Choose \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project\u003c/strong>, select your original \u003cstrong>Metroid (U).nes\u003c/strong>, choose \u003cstrong>Prepare recovered resources (partial)\u003c/strong>, then \u003cstrong>Import as new project\u003c/strong>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In \u003cstrong>Area library \u2192 Investigated source\u003c/strong>, choose an area and source room; open its recovered page in \u003cstrong>Scene\u003c/strong>. Inspect Brinstar, Norfair, Tourian, Kraid hideout and Ridley hideout separately.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>In \u003cstrong>NATIVE ROOM STRUCTURES\u003c/strong>, choose an existing object. Its label shows the native 16-pixel X/Y position, current structure index and palette. Choose another native structure ID and click \u003cstrong>Replace selected structure\u003c/strong>. This control replaces a whole structure, not a single tile.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confirm the visible change. Undo and Redo. Use \u003cstrong>Save Project As\u003c/strong>, reopen the saved project, then \u003cstrong>Build &amp; Run\u003c/strong>. Also download the rebuilt ROM for an external emulator check.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reimport the rebuilt ROM. Confirm the intended room edit remains. Verify the actual affected room in gameplay; opening the source page does not demonstrate a safe route to it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Try an unsupported ordinary Scene/CHR edit. It must remain savable and block native output until undone. Native structure replacement is the supported edit for this checkpoint.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"metroid-verification-and-limits\">Verification and limits\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Metroid_Verification.json\u003c/code> records five passing verification groups, including every original room's tile and attribute RAM against the original 6502 loaders, all five initial graphics/palette states, intake, Undo/Redo, save/reopen, rebuilding, reimport, aliases and rejection guards. The supported example edit changes one byte. The original 6502 loader also reproduces all rooms from that edited ROM.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>targeted-gate/Summary.json\u003c/code> records the focused compatibility run against the exact final HTML hash. The earlier full gate was intentionally stopped at 63 suites / 1,393 checks before Metroid integration. Those partial results are historical and do not certify this build. A fresh full release regression run is not claimed.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Real browser appearance, file downloads, controllers, mobile touch, listening and full gameplay traversal require owner/device acceptance. Background room recovery and structure substitution are partial support. World-map placement and travel, menus, actors, items, doors, collision-rule editing, animation, dynamic palette states, native Audio writing and complete behavior recovery remain unfinished. No title in the 17-title batch is complete.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"metroid-metroid-audio-update\">Metroid Audio update\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>The AUDIO / ROM Audio Library now offers 12 music cues, 24 SFX and one reset control. It restores automatically from the original source when a recovered Metroid project is opened or reopened. See \u003ccode>METROID_AUDIO.md\u003c/code> for steps, revision limits and the distinction between a tracker excerpt and original-engine audio.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"metroid-source-and-continuation\">Source and continuation\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Developer/Current_Source.zip\u003c/code> contains every current bundle input, the deterministic bundler, a standalone built HTML, focused tests and verification scripts. Extract it, run \u003ccode>python3 app/tools/bundle.py --check\u003c/code>, and run \u003ccode>node app/tests/native-metroid-resources.cjs\u003c/code>. Private native-loader verification additionally requires the owner's original ROM directory via \u003ccode>FORGE_CORPUS_DIR\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The source archive is a current rebuildable snapshot; historical Git objects and the complete historical regression fixtures remain in the recovered full repository. \u003ccode>Developer/Recovery_2_Source.patch\u003c/code> is a supplementary patch against recovered baseline \u003ccode>7c66042c8dde8621f042581421a584bfd959e6ea\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>The original tutorial, documentation and earlier evidence remain included in the documented kit. Review Metroid first; do not start Lolo or another title until the owner selects the next project.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/native-resource-recovery-2/METROID_REVIEW.md", "sourceSha256": "d3ca00fb36baec50369909be7ef51ffd174fbfac7ac14aee2034ad265abd126d"}, {"id": "metroid-audio", "title": "Metroid Audio workflow", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"metroid-audio-metroid-audio\">Metroid Audio\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Open the original Metroid (U) ROM through \u003cstrong>Open ROM / project \u2192 Open source Audio catalog\u003c/strong>, or prepare/import its recovered project and switch to \u003cstrong>AUDIO \u2192 ROM Audio Library\u003c/strong>. Opening or reopening the recovered project restores the Audio source automatically. Brinstar is selected first.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Choose one of 12 music cues or 24 SFX in \u003cstrong>Native audio cue\u003c/strong>. The additional Silence/reset entry is a control, so playback/conversion is disabled for it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Inspect cue\u003c/strong>, then browse Native commands, volume envelopes and source locations. Music has 41 physical channel streams across six banks. Procedural SFX have handler/request evidence; their timed notes come from execution.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Play native\u003c/strong> to audition the original driver for 1\u20131024 updates. Title/ending music uses bank 0; area music uses its declared bank. Playback uses cleared driver RAM, original startup random seeds $11/$FF and one native random-number update each frame. This repeatable context is not a gameplay recording. Stop ends the preview.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose \u003cstrong>Preview editable score\u003c/strong>, review the differences, then \u003cstrong>Import into tracker\u003c/strong> and confirm. This appends a finite excerpt and replaces the music order/tempo for music imports. Use \u003cstrong>Tracker\u003c/strong> to edit; one Undo restores the previous project. Save Project As preserves the adaptation. Single-channel SFX import requires a short, representable selection; other effects can be previewed as music excerpts.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\n\u003cp>The installed profile matches the supplied US revision, SHA-256 \u003ccode>c5eea06e1e1128b576bd789f1a4f63bb154d6d31579c2f319382fb77a72d34a6\u003c/code>. Different revisions, hacks and rebuilt ROMs with changed bytes do not silently reuse this profile. A recovered project's preserved original ROM remains the catalog source after room edits.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Native playback executes the original driver through Forge's shared CPU/APU. Static tables are decoded from the current source, with bounded stream walking and explicit music-end/loop boundaries. Human cue names and request routing follow the reviewed Metroid disassembly. Source reference: nmikstas/metroid-disassembly revision 4270d57, banks 01/07, B3B4/BBFA/BD31/BADC/BB1C and C000.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Tracker import is a frame-sampled adaptation.\u003c/strong> It preserves neither original engine commands nor sub-frame articulation. Nearest-pitch tuning, envelope adaptation, finite-window cuts and unsupported short-noise/DPCM are disclosed in the preview. It does not import whole song loop structure or gameplay triggers.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Audio writing back into the original Metroid ROM remains unsupported.\u003c/strong> If a tracker adaptation changes a native room project's Audio, native output must refuse it until undone. Keep an adaptation in a separate saved project if continuing native room edits. Native structure replacement remains the supported ROM writer.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Verification: \u003ccode>Metroid_Audio_Public_Tests.json\u003c/code> uses independent synthetic fixtures. \u003ccode>Metroid_Audio_Verification.json\u003c/code> records all 36 playable supplied-ROM cues, high-bit-first SFX dispatch, source guards, source restoration, preview/import and Undo/Redo. \u003ccode>audio-gate/Summary.json\u003c/code> records the focused shared compatibility suites. No full release regression or listening/hardware equivalence is claimed. Browser checks are explicitly blocked because this runtime has no Chromium executable; desktop/mobile controls still need owner acceptance.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>Reproduction: \u003ccode>python3 app/tools/bundle.py --check\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>node app/tests/metroid-audio.cjs\u003c/code>. Supplied-input checks: \u003ccode>node reviews/native-resource-recovery-2/verify_metroid_audio.cjs /path/to/Metroid.nes /path/to/report.json\u003c/code>. No ROM, extracted music or sample payload is included in this kit or the source checkpoint.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/native-resource-recovery-2/METROID_AUDIO.md", "sourceSha256": "de65e4b300cbf6f5eb8cc4502bd8f64a7abb1c7874a247814a3e87a454651bea"}, {"id": "lolo", "title": "Lolo room acceptance", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"lolo-lolo-owner-acceptance\">Lolo owner acceptance\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Automated results do not check these boxes.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] Import the exact original Lolo ROM and open an assembled source room.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Inspect rooms 1, 25, 41 and 50, including the lava palette states.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Replace room 1's boulder at column 2, row 2 with Floor.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Check canvas brush and keyboard/select controls.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Undo, Redo, Save Project As, close and reopen.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Build, download and reimport; confirm the intended source room edit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Verify the modified room in actual gameplay or another emulator.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Open distant rooms and return; retain earlier native recipes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm player/enemy/chest/border/setting cells refuse terrain edits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm an over-capacity edit refuses atomically.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm unsupported ordinary CHR edits remain saved and block native output.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Record failures by source room, row, column, selected code and action.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>Audio follow-up: use ../lolo-audio-1/Owner_Checklist.md for the installed Lolo catalog and current Scene + Audio verification. Moving sprites, menus, animation, live HUD and full gameplay traversal remain separate work. The next title waits for owner selection.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/lolo-recovery-1/Owner_Checklist.md", "sourceSha256": "e48040e338efd92707dd5e865126256dd5b6c3c84614049a56aae81702e7ed18"}, {"id": "lolo-audio", "title": "Lolo Audio acceptance", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"lolo-audio-lolo-scene-audio-acceptance\">Lolo Scene + Audio acceptance\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Automated checks do not check these owner boxes.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] Open the updated app; import your exact original Lolo ROM or reopen your saved native Lolo project.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm the assembled rooms still look correct.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio \u2192 ROM Audio Library shows Adventures of Lolo, 34 cues and Music 01 selected.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Inspect a music cue, play it, stop it and listen for glitches; repeat with SFX.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Preview/import music; check tracker rows, Undo/Redo and Save Project As/reopen.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Try SFX 01 as Single-channel SFX using 16 native updates.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm tracker audio edits are preserved but native builds refuse without an audio encoder. Undo the import to resume native room rebuilding.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Edit room 1's boulder at column 2, row 2 to Floor; save/reopen, build and open the derivative ROM. Confirm Audio still finds all 34 cues.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Play the edited room and its music in gameplay or another emulator.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Report source filename/hash, cue, frame window, action and browser for any failure.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>Every next project must check Audio using ../PROJECT_VERIFICATION.md before closing its verification checkpoint. Missing drivers stay explicitly open.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/lolo-audio-1/Owner_Checklist.md", "sourceSha256": "1d91b299993ac03b6f9e3f00cd444ca94b44c0431cebb2256e10cc8d504325db"}, {"id": "river-city", "title": "River City Scene and Audio acceptance", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"river-city-river-city-owner-acceptance\">River City owner acceptance\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Automated checks do not check these boxes.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] Open the updated app and import your exact original River City Ransom ROM.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Inspect street locations 0, 7, 21 and 32 through Area library. The view is a 20-row crop of the native background, with original CHR/palette state.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm an interior/special location reports unavailable instead of showing a substituted street or CHR sheet.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Replace a native tile using the row/column controls; repeat with the canvas brush. Check shared aliases in another segment.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Undo/Redo, Save Project As, close/reopen, build and reimport.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Play the edited street in the native engine or another emulator; check the intended visual change and its native aliases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio \u2192 ROM Audio Library shows 63 entries and defaults to Music 02.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Inspect, play and stop several music/SFX selections; listen for glitches or timing problems.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Preview/import music, Undo/Redo and save/reopen the tracker rows.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Try SFX 20 using 16 native updates and Single-channel SFX.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Verify tracker edits are preserved but native Audio writing refuses. Undo the Audio import before building a street edit.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Rebuild a street edit and reopen that ROM; confirm its Audio catalog and native playback remain available.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Report source hash, location/segment, cell, cue/window, action and browser with any issue.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>The remaining 22 special/interior locations and other unrecovered domains remain open. Review this checkpoint before selecting another title. Audio is mandatory for each project's verification; see ../PROJECT_VERIFICATION.md.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/river-city-1/Owner_Checklist.md", "sourceSha256": "8d4d1a4d036a94ad24b9a4651d495e27c32ea0277ec18a63e15b098ace6de492"}, {"id": "shadowgate", "title": "Shadowgate picture and Audio acceptance", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"shadowgate-shadowgate-owner-review\">Shadowgate owner review\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Extract NF6.zip into a short folder such as C:\\NF6. Open NF/START\u003cem>HERE.html, then this kit's NES\u003c/em>Forge.html. Use your own original Shadowgate ROM.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] Import as recovered resources. Area library \u2192 Investigated source lists 130 picture slots. Confirm representative pictures, including 0, 75, 76 and 129. Only the 112\u00d7112 crop belongs to the resource; objects/doors and room-state overlays remain unresolved.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Select an original native tile ID, replace a cell, Undo/Redo, Save Project As, reopen, Build &amp; Run, and reimport the output. Confirm the edited picture and unchanged surrounding native behavior. The legacy source header remains unchanged.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Open Audio \u2192 ROM Audio Library immediately after import and again after reopening. Confirm 35 entries and the installed Shadowgate driver. Request 13 is selected initially.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Inspect and listen to representative music requests and SFX. Request 24 must remain inspectable with playback/conversion disabled because its standalone header is invalid. Automated PCM/register checks do not replace this listening step.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Preview editable music, review the listed adaptation losses, import, Undo/Redo, save/reopen and audition the tracker. For a short single-channel effect try request 21 with 16 updates and Single-channel SFX.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Confirm native building refuses after importing editable Audio; Undo the import to resume native picture rebuilding. No native Audio encoder is available.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Rebuild a native picture edit and confirm the Audio driver/catalog still loads and auditions from the rebuilt file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Record browser, OS, controller/touch and full-game findings separately. No automated report establishes these acceptances.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>Open domains: alternate room/palette states, object/door overlays, UI/text, puzzle logic and interactions, animation, full gameplay and native Audio writing.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/shadowgate-1/Owner_Checklist.md", "sourceSha256": "dbe1d12de3afb1429b1619686a855002a3dd2c44700889acbad482c609a5e314"}, {"id": "verification", "title": "Per-title verification requirements", "html": "\u003ch1 id=\"verification-per-project-recovery-verification\">Per-project recovery verification\u003c/h1>\n\n\u003cp>Owner requirement, 2026-10-23: check the audio driver for every project as part of verification. A correct Scene alone does not close a project checkpoint.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cp>For each supplied title and revision, record the exact source identity and the current app hash. Keep separate results for Scene and Audio. Missing or unsupported audio remains an explicit open item; do not silently mark that project verified or move to the next title without reporting it.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] Scene: assembled native placement, graphics and palettes match the source.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Scene lifecycle: edit, Undo/Redo, save/reopen, native build and derivative reimport.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio detection: the exact source has an installed driver/catalog, or an explicit unresolved result with a reason.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio inventory: music/SFX cue identities, physical banks, channels and instrument/sample evidence are checked against actual bytes; names are not guessed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio native execution: every available cue produces bounded native APU activity; check representative music and SFX by listening in the owner browser. Automated PCM checks do not replace listening.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio workflow: Audio tab loads the catalog directly after project import and after save/reopen; cue inspection and native preview work.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Editable Audio: where available, preview/import representative music and SFX through Undo/Redo and save/reopen; report adaptation losses and conversion refusals explicitly.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Combined preservation: a native Scene edit/rebuild retains audio bytes and driver detection. When derivatives are supported, prove their audio identity independently of a filename or original full-ROM hash.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio writing: report whether edited scores can be encoded into the original native driver. A canonical excerpt alone is not native audio writing; unsupported native builds must refuse.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Retained compatibility: existing importers, Audio and project persistence keep their checks. Report the tested scope and any outstanding browser, controller or gameplay checks.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>A missing decoder blocks an Audio pass. Existing unsupported audio writers and other partial domains must be disclosed. Whole-game recovery remains separate from bounded Scene and Audio verification.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003ch2 id=\"verification-per-title-tutorial-requirement\">Per-title tutorial requirement\u003c/h2>\n\n\u003cp>Owner requirement, 2026-10-24: each imported/supported title needs instructions for its own methods, alongside the general NES Forge tutorial. Use \u003ccode>app/docs/projects/PROJECT_TUTORIAL_TEMPLATE.md\u003c/code> and the current \u003ccode>PUNCH_OUT.md\u003c/code> lesson. Older guides awaiting expansion are listed in \u003ccode>app/docs/projects/README.md\u003c/code>; their inclusion does not establish a new acceptance pass.\u003c/p>\n\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>[ ] Exact input/application identities and a reproducible supported edit with actual control labels.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Per-domain asset inventory, source ownership, native writer scope and explicit gaps.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Format lesson: bank mapping, pointers, compression, raster context, aliases and capacity.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Independent RE, assembly and external hex workflows, plus source/HTML module connections.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Separate native rebuilt-ROM, authored-game and customized standalone-HTML outcomes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Audio steps, Undo/Redo, save/reopen, native rebuild/reimport and original-engine checks.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>[ ] Reproduction commands and clearly separated automated evidence and learner/device acceptance.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\n\u003cp>Update the title lesson in the same checkpoint that changes its method. A tutorial can be complete for a bounded supported workflow while unrecovered domains stay open; importing a ROM is not whole-game recovery.\u003c/p>\n", "source": "reviews/PROJECT_VERIFICATION.md", "sourceSha256": "76415ea4669bdf412325a0210447bee2804c4daf2892fe7fa11ce1737789c929"}, {"id": "synthetic", "title": "Runnable synthetic codec exercise", "html": "\u003cp class=\"part\">Runnable synthetic exercise\u003c/p>\u003ch1>Synthetic codec source\u003c/h1>\u003cp>Save this as \u003ccode>native-codec-exercise.cjs\u003c/code> and run \u003ccode>node native-codec-exercise.cjs\u003c/code>. This is an invented 48-byte teaching format, not an NES ROM or a Punch-Out decoder.\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>&#x27;use strict&#x27;;\n// Synthetic teaching format, authored for this tutorial. This is NOT an NES\n// game decoder or evidence that any commercial title is supported.\nconst assert = require(&#x27;node:assert/strict&#x27;);\n\n// File layout: 16-byte header, 16-byte RLE storage, 16 protected sentinel bytes.\n// Header [0..3] identifies this format; [4..5] is a little-endian data pointer.\n// Each stream pair is [count, tile], with count=0 terminating the stream.\nconst DATA = 16, CAPACITY = 16, CELLS = 8;\nfunction decode(rom) {\n  assert.equal(rom.length, 48);\n  assert.deepEqual([...rom.slice(0, 4)], [0x4e, 0x46, 0x45, 1]);\n  const start = rom[4] | rom[5] &lt;&lt; 8;\n  assert.equal(start, DATA);\n  const cells = [];\n  let ended = false;\n  for (let p = start; p &lt; DATA + CAPACITY;) {\n    const count = rom[p++];\n    if (!count) { ended = true; break; }\n    assert(p &lt; DATA + CAPACITY, &#x27;Missing RLE operand&#x27;);\n    const tile = rom[p++];\n    assert(cells.length + count &lt;= CELLS, &#x27;RLE overrun&#x27;);\n    cells.push(...Array(count).fill(tile));\n  }\n  assert(ended &amp;&amp; cells.length === CELLS, &#x27;Missing terminator or incomplete room&#x27;);\n  return {width: 4, height: 2, cells};\n}\nfunction encode(source, room) {\n  decode(source); // Validate the source before deriving a new candidate.\n  assert.equal(room.cells.length, CELLS);\n  assert(room.cells.every(n =&gt; Number.isInteger(n) &amp;&amp; n &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; n &lt;= 255));\n  const stream = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i &lt; CELLS;) {\n    let count = 1;\n    while (i + count &lt; CELLS &amp;&amp; room.cells[i + count] === room.cells[i]) count++;\n    stream.push(count, room.cells[i]);\n    i += count;\n  }\n  stream.push(0);\n  assert(stream.length &lt;= CAPACITY, &#x27;Fixed native storage capacity exceeded&#x27;);\n  const candidate = source.slice();\n  candidate.set(stream, DATA); // Do not rewrite the header or protected bytes.\n  assert.deepEqual(decode(candidate).cells, room.cells);\n  for (let i = 0; i &lt; source.length; i++) {\n    if (i &lt; DATA || i &gt;= DATA + CAPACITY) assert.equal(candidate[i], source[i]);\n  }\n  return candidate;\n}\nfunction exercise() {\n  const source = new Uint8Array(48).fill(0xaa);\n  source.set([0x4e, 0x46, 0x45, 1, DATA, 0], 0);\n  source.fill(0, DATA, DATA + CAPACITY);\n  source.set([4, 1, 4, 2, 0], DATA);\n  const frozen = source.slice();\n  const room = decode(source);\n  assert.deepEqual(room.cells, [1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2]);\n  room.cells[1] = 3;\n  const built = encode(source, room);\n  assert.deepEqual(decode(built), room);\n  assert.deepEqual(source, frozen, &#x27;The source must remain unchanged&#x27;);\n  const saved = JSON.stringify({source: [...source], room});\n  const reopened = JSON.parse(saved);\n  assert.deepEqual(encode(Uint8Array.from(reopened.source), reopened.room), built);\n  assert.deepEqual(encode(built, decode(built)), built, &#x27;Unchanged derivative is byte-identical&#x27;);\n  assert.throws(() =&gt; encode(source, {cells: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]}), /capacity/);\n  const invalid = source.slice(); invalid[4] = 47;\n  assert.throws(() =&gt; decode(invalid));\n  console.log(&#x27;Synthetic codec exercise passed: decode, edit, encode, reopen, re-import, bounds, protected bytes.&#x27;);\n}\nif (require.main === module) exercise();\nmodule.exports = {decode, encode, exercise};\n\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>", "source": "app/docs/native-codec-exercise.cjs", "sourceSha256": "f43d7d5cd905154c8a95e603b7a7135d3af356ca3eacb58c79652770462b3cb8"}]});
